body:
[Definition def:IndS of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We call the following setup the \emph{independent setting}: 
		Let the underlying point process have independent increments and is thus either given by a Poisson point process, cf.~[LastPenrose2017], or by a Bernoulli site-percolated lattice. Further, for a given collection of mutually different vertex pairs \(\{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2}\},\dots,\{\boldsymbol{x}_{k_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\}\), we have 
	\begin{equation*}\tag{Ind}
\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\Big(\bigcap_{j=1}^k \{\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1}\sim \boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}\} \, \Big| \, \mathscr{V} \Big) = \prod_{i=1}^k \mathbf{p}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}), 
	\end{equation*} 
	for a suitable, symmetric function \(\mathbf{p}\). Here, \(\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\) denotes the law where the given points \(\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\) have been added to the graph in the Poisson point process case, or the law conditioned on the event that said vertices survived the site percolation, respectively.

[Proposition prop:equivalence_nonlinear_mp_weak_sol_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ be a probability measure on $(C_{\infty}, \mathcal{B} )$, such that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{P}_{\nu}(x_t \in d y) = v_t(y) dy, 
 \qquad t> 0 ,
\end{equation}
and such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto b\big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x)\big], \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto \sigma \sigma^\top \big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^d).
\end{equation}

Then $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ is a solution to D-NL-MP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ if and only if there exists a (weak) solution $X$ to D-MKV$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ such that $[X] =\mathbb{P}_{\nu} $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

