[Definition def:admissible of arXiv:2601.19630, 'Mass generation for the two dimensional O(N) Linear Sigma Model in the large N limit'] We will say that a cylindrical functional $F$ is \textit{admissible} provided at least one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item All first derivatives of $G$ are bounded.
\item All second derivatives of $G$ are bounded and $\mathbb{E}^{\mu_{m_{*}}}|\nabla G(Z)|^{2}<\infty$ for $Z \sim \mu_{m_{*}}$.
\end{enumerate}

[Corollary subseq-c of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let $\{n_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty }$ be a strictly increasing
sequence with $n_{k}\rightarrow \infty $, such that $c_{n_{k}}\rightarrow
c^{\ast }\in (0,1)$. Then there exists a subsequential limit $T^{\ast }$ of
the inverses $\{T_{n_{k}-1}\}_{k=0}^{\infty }$ such that $c^{\ast }$ is the
unique solution in $(0,1)$ of 
\begin{equation}
\int_{0}^{c^{\ast }}w(T^{\ast }(u))\,du=c^{\ast }\int_{0}^{1}w(T^{\ast
}(u))\,du.  
\end{equation}
In particular, for this fixed subsequence and its associated limit $T^{\ast
} $, the interior crossing points $c_{n_{k}}$ cannot converge to two
different values.

[Proposition 35 (Multiplication between $n$th and $m$th chaos) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] Assume $f\in\H^{\otimes n}$ and $g\in\H^{\otimes m}$. Then 
\begin{equation}

 \hat I_n(f)\hat I_m(g) 
 = \sum_{p=0}^{n\wedge m} \hat I_{n+m-2p}(f\star_p g)
\end{equation} 
where $\star_0 = \otimes$ and 
the contraction $\star_p$ is defined 
for $\mathbf{i} = (i_1,\dots,i_{n-p})$ 
and $\mathbf{j} = (j_1,\dots,j_{m-p})$ 
by  
\begin{equation}

 (f\star_pg)(\mathbf{i},\mathbf{j}) 
 = \sum_{\substack{\Sigma\in\frakS(p,n)\\ \bar\Sigma\in\frakS(p,m)}}
 \sum_{\sigma\in\frakS(p)}
 \sum_{\mathbf{k}\in\intset{1,N}^p} f(\Sigma(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{i}))g(\bar\Sigma(\mathbf{k},\sigma(\mathbf{j})))\;. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 2 of arXiv:2606.14917, 'The Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process on $\scr P_2$ \\with a volatility operator'] The symmetric, non-negative bilinear form
\begin{equation}
	\E^{G,\lam}(u,v):=\int_{T_\lam}{\big\la  Du(\lam\circ f^{-1})\circ f,A^{-1}\big(Du(\lam\circ f^{-1})\circ f\big)\big\ra}_{T_\lam}G(\d f),\qquad u,v\in C_b^1(\scr P_2),
\end{equation}
is a pre-Dirichlet form in $L^2(\scr P_2,G\circ\Psi_\lam^{-1})$, i.e.~it has the Markov property and is closable.

