[Definition Union of diagrammatic graphs of arXiv:2506.06559, 'Bi-infinite incipient cluster in high dimensions'] Let ${\bf G}=(G,S,\ell)$ be a diagrammatic graph and $x,y\in \mathbb{Z}^d$. We will denote ${\bf G}\uplus \{x,y\}$ the diagrammatic graph $(G’,S’,\ell’)$ defined as follows
\begin{enumerate}
\item if $x,y\in \{\ell(s), s\in S\}$, then 
\begin{itemize}
 \item $V(G’)=V(G)$ 
 \item $E(G’)=E(G)\cup e_{\{x,y\}}$ where an edge $e_{\{x,y\}}$ is added between the vertices $s,s’\in S$ such that $x=\ell(s)$ and $y=\ell(s’)$ 
  \item $S=S’$ and $\ell=\ell’$.
\end{itemize}
\item if $x\in \{\ell(s), s\in S\}$ and $y\notin  \{\ell(s), s\in S\}$,
\begin{itemize}
 \item $V(G’)=V(G)\cup\{y_*\}$ 
 \item $E(G’)=E(G)\cup e_{\{x,y\}}$ where an edge $e_{\{x,y\}}$ is added between $y_*$ and the vertex $s\in S$ such that $x=\ell(s)$
  \item $S’=S \cup \{y_*\}$, $\ell(s)=\ell’(s)$ if $s\in S$ and $\ell’(y_*)=y$.
\end{itemize}
\item if $x,y\notin \{\ell(s), s\in S\}$
\begin{itemize}
 \item $V(G’)=V(G)\cup\{x_*,y_*\}$ 
 \item $E(G’)=E(G)\cup e_{\{x,y\}}$ where an edge $e_{\{x,y\}}$ is added between $x_*$ and $y_*$.
  \item $S’=S \cup \{x_*,y_*\}$, $\ell(s)=\ell’(s)$ if $s\in S$, $\ell’(x_*)=x$ and $\ell’(y_*)=y$.
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

We will extend this notation to ${\bf G}\uplus {\bf G}'$ when ${\bf G}$ is a diagrammatic graph and ${\bf G}'$ a fully labeled graph simply, by successively adding to ${\bf G}$ the finite number of labeled edges composing ${\bf G}'$.

[Lemma lem:first-failure-short of arXiv:2608.05123, 'A central limit theorem for the random assignment problem'] Conditional on \(U\) under either reference law,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(D_i\ge d\mid U)=
 \frac{S_{i,U}(d)-S_{i,U}(n-)}
      {S_{i,U}(0)-S_{i,U}(n-)},\qquad 0\le d<n.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, if independently for \(j\ne i\),
\[
 \Xi_{ij}\sim{\rm Unif}[-U_j,n-U_j],\qquad
 M_i=\min_{j\ne i}\Xi_{ij},
\]
then
\begin{equation}
 A_i\stackrel d=M_i\mid\{-U_i\le M_i<n-U_i\}.
\end{equation}

If \(J_i\) is the almost surely unique index attaining this minimum,
then, for almost every \(d\) with positive row density,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(J_i=j\mid U,D_i=d)
 =\frac{\omega_n(d+U_j-U_i)}
 {\sum_{k\ne i}\omega_n(d+U_k-U_i)}.
\end{equation}
The row variables are independent conditional on \(U\).

[Proposition 32 (The dimension of the support and the range of SBM) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be a non-zero finite measure, let $\gamma > 0$ and let $(\mathcal{X}_s, s \geq 0)$ be an $\text{SBM}(\gamma)$ started from $\mu$. Then we have that for $y>0$, that
\begin{equation*}
    \text{dim}(\text{supp}(\mathcal{X}_y)) = 2 \wedge d \hspace{0.5cm} \text{ 
  on   } \{\mathcal{X}_y \neq 0\} \text{    a.s.}
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, for $\delta>0$ we have that
\begin{equation*}
    \text{dim}\Big(\overline{\bigcup_{y \geq \delta}\text{supp}(\mathcal{X}_y)}\Big) = 4 \wedge d \hspace{0.5cm} \text{ 
  on   } \{\mathcal{X}_{\delta} \neq 0\} \text{    a.s.}
\end{equation*}
Finally we have that
\begin{equation*}
    \text{dim}(\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{X})) = 4 \wedge d \hspace{0.5cm} \text{a.s.}
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] Assume that Assumption \ref{assumption:ass1} holds. Let $T_M^{(n)}$ be the stopping time given in \eqref{eq:stopping-time}. Then we have 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T_M^{(n)}\wedge T_M^{(m)}]} \|\Delta_{n,m} [U_t]\|_2\to 0
\end{equation}
as $n,m\to \infty.$

