[Proposition eq:cocycle of arXiv:2608.28278, 'Reduction of symmetric time-dependent Hamiltonian systems I: presymplectic principal $\R$-'] The $1$-form $\eta$ is $G$-invariant if and only if there exists a multiplicative function $m: G \to \mathbb{R}$ such that
$$\Pi\circ \phi_g= \Pi + m(g), \quad \text{for all} \quad g\in G.$$
As a consequence, there exists $c_\phi : \mathfrak{g} \to \mathbb{R}$, $\mathfrak{g}$ being the Lie algebra of $G$, given by
\begin{equation}
        c_\phi = dm(e) \in \mathfrak{g}^*,
\end{equation}
which satisfies,
\begin{equation}
    c_\phi[\xi, \eta]=0, \quad \text{for all} \quad \xi, \eta \in \mathfrak{g},
\end{equation}
that is, $c_\phi \in \mathfrak{g}^*$ is a $1$-cocycle of $\mathfrak{g}$.

