    d) The same holds true if in \eqref{eq:J_mu_nu_def} one replaces 

    $(R_\rho,D_\rho)$ by $(\hat{R}_\rho,\hat{D}_\rho)$, for $\rho = \mu$ and/or $\rho = \nu$.

[Lemma lem:billingsley of arXiv:2607.04002, 'Collisions of random walks in unimodular random graphs: applications to the random geometr'] Let~$\{N_i: i \in \mathbb{N}\}$ be an i.i.d. family of Poisson random variables with mean~$\lambda > 0$. There exists~$c_\mathrm{pois} = c_\mathrm{pois}(\lambda) > 0$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P \left( \sum^m_{i=1}N^2_i \ge c_\mathrm{pois} \cdot m \right) \leq \frac{1}{m^2} \quad \text{for any } m \in \mathbb{N}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:tail_coup of arXiv:2605.10491, 'Zero-couplings of infinite measures with cyclically monotone support and multivariate regu'] Let $P$ and $Q$ in $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be regularly varying with auxiliary functions $b_1$ and $b_2$, indices $\alpha_1 > 0$ and $\alpha_2 > 0$, and limit measures $\mu$ and $\nu$ in $\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, respectively. Let $\psi$ be a closed convex function such $\partial\psi$ contains the cyclically monotone support of some $\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{cm}}(P,Q)$. Every sequence of positive numbers $t_n \to \infty$ then contains a subsequence such that, as $n \to \infty$ along the subsequence, we have writing $B(t) = \operatorname{diag}(b_1(t) 1_d, b_2(t) 1_d)$,
    \begin{align}

        t_n \operatorname{res}\pi(B(t_n) \,\cdot\,) 
        &\raisebox{-0.5pt}{\,\scriptsize$\stackrel{\raisebox{-0.5pt}{\mbox{\tiny $0$}}}{\longrightarrow}$}\, \gamma 
        &&\text{in $\mathrm{M}_{0}(\reals^d\times\reals^d)$}, \\
        \notag
        B(t_n)^{-1} \partial \psi

        &\raisebox{-0.5pt}{\,\scriptsize$\stackrel{\raisebox{-0.5pt}{\mbox{\tiny $\mathcal{F}$}}}{\longrightarrow}$}\, \partial\bar{\psi} 
        &&\text{in $\mathcal{F}(\reals^d\times\reals^d)$}, 
    \end{align}
	for some $\gamma \in \Gamma_{0,\mathrm{cm}}(\mu, \nu)$ and some closed convex function $\bar{\psi}$ satisfying $\operatorname{spt} \gamma \subset \partial \bar{\psi}$ and $\bar{\psi}(0) = \inf_{x\in\mathbb{R}^d} \bar{\psi}(x) < \infty$.
    If $\Gamma_{0,\mathrm{cm}}(\mu, \nu)$ is a singleton, say $\left\{\gamma\right\}$, then we actually have $t \operatorname{res} \pi(B(t) \,\cdot\,) \raisebox{-0.5pt}{\,\scriptsize$\stackrel{\raisebox{-0.5pt}{\mbox{\tiny $0$}}}{\longrightarrow}$}\, \gamma$ as $t\to\infty$, and, with $E = \operatorname{diag}(\alpha_{1}^{-1} 1_d, \alpha_{2}^{-1} 1_d)$, the measure $\gamma$ satisfies
    \begin{equation}

        \forall \lambda > 0, \qquad \gamma(\lambda^{-E}\,\cdot\,) = \lambda \gamma(\,\cdot\,);
    \end{equation}
    in particular, its support satisfies $\lambda^E \operatorname{spt} \gamma = \operatorname{spt} \gamma$ for all $\lambda > 0$.

