				D(\SCL_1, \SCL_2) = t + D( \SCL_2, \SCB_t(\SCL_1)). 
			\end{equation*}
			The same is true with a deterministic connected arc (resp.~two deterministic and disjoint connected arcs) of $\partial \BD$ in place of $\SCL_1$ (resp.~$\SCL_1$ and $\SCL_2$).
			\item Let $\SCL_1, \SCL_2 \in \Gamma$. Then, the compact set $K_{\SCL_1, \SCL_2}$ defined as the closure of $ \{ z \in \overline{\BD}, \ D(\SCL_1, z)+ D(\SCL_2,z)= D(\SCL_1, \SCL_2) \}$ contains a connected component $\widetilde{K}_{\SCL_1, \SCL_2} $ that contains both $\SCL_1$ and $\SCL_2$. The same is true with a deterministic connected arc (resp.~two deterministic and disjoint connected arcs) of $\partial\BD$ in place of $\SCL_1$ (resp.~$\SCL_1$ and $\SCL_2$). 
