[Definition 80 (Bell polynomials) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The Wick map $\W(t,x)$ is the generating function of Bell polynomials, 
in the sense that 
\begin{equation}
 \W(t,x) = \sum_{n\geqs0} B_n(x, -y_2, \dots, -y_n) \frac{t^n}{n!}\;.
\end{equation} 
$B_n$ is called the \emph{$n$th complete Bell polynomial}. It can be decomposed 
as 
\begin{equation}
 B_n(x, -y_2, \dots, -y_n)
 = \sum_{k=1}^n B_{n,k}(x, -y_2, \dots, -y_{n-k+1})\;,
\end{equation} 
where each $B_{n,k}$ is the homogeneous part of degree $k$ of $B_n$, and 
is called \emph{incomplete Bell polynomial} of order $(n,k)$.

[Lemma 69 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Suppose that $\beta \gg n (\log n)^\lambda$ for $\lambda = \lambda(n) \rightarrow \infty$ arbitrarily slowly. Then for any fixed $r \geq 1$
    \begin{equation*}
         \E \Big[ \bP \big( B_\cT(1,r) \neq B_M(1,r) \big) \Big] = o(1).
    \end{equation*}

