body:
[Satz thm:LSICone of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] For any real $p \ge 2$, $\mu_{p,n}$ satisfies an $\mathrm{LS}_q$-inequality with constant of order $n^{-1/(p-1)}$. More precisely, for any smooth enough $f \colon {\mathbb S}_p^{n-1} \to {\mathbb R}$, it holds that
	\begin{align*}
		\mathrm{Ent}_{\mu_{p,n}}(|f|^q) &\le 4^q q^{q-1} \frac {\Gamma(\frac{n-q}{p})}{p^{q/p}\Gamma(\frac n p)} \int_{{\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}} \lvert \nabla_{S} f(\theta) \rvert_q^q d \mu_{p,n}\\
		&\le 3 \cdot 4^q q^{q-1} n^{-\frac{1}{p-1}} \int_{{\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}} \lvert \nabla_S f(\theta) \rvert_q^q d \mu_{p,n},
	\end{align*}
	where the second inequality is valid for any $n \ge 3$. The same inequalities remain true if we assume $f$ to be defined and smooth in a neighbourhood of ${\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}$ and replace $\nabla_S f$ by the usual (non-intrinsic) gradient $\nabla f$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

