[Theorem thm:lehner-rad of arXiv:2606.14687, 'Lehner's operator norm formulas, semidefinite programming, and spiked matrix models'] {Lehner-1999-NormFreeOperatorMatrixCoefficients}}]

    Suppose $(\mathfrak{r}_i)_{i = 1}^n$ is a free Rademacher family of operators on a suitable Hilbert space and let $\mathfrak{1}$ be the identity operator on that space.
    Then,
    \begin{align*}
      &\Lambda_{\mathsf{rad}}(\bm A_0, \bm A_1, \dots, \bm A_n) \\
      &\hspace{1cm}\colonequals \lambda_{\max}\left(\bm A_0 \otimes \mathfrak{1} + \sum_{i = 1}^n \bm A_i \otimes \mathfrak{r}_i\right) \\
      &\hspace{1cm}= \inf_{\substack{\bm Z \in \mathbb{C}^{d \times d}_{\mathsf{herm}} \\ \bm Z \succ \bm 0}} \lambda_{\max}\left(\bm Z + \bm A_0 + \sum_{i = 1}^n \frac{1}{2} \bm Z^{1/2}((\bm I_d + 4(\bm Z^{-1/2}\bm A_i \bm Z^{-1/2})^2)^{1/2} - \bm I_d)\bm Z^{1/2}\right). \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
    \end{align*}
    Further, the infimum in (equation eq:lehner-rad of the paper) can be restricted to those $\bm Z$ for which the expression inside $\lambda_{\max}(\cdot)$ equals a scalar multiple of $\bm I_d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2605.30616, 'Brownian motion: the hyperbolic number setting'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{C})$ and $P_{1}$ and
$P_{2}$ as above, and let $T$ be a $\mathbb{H}$-valued random variable,
with idempotent decomposition 
\begin{equation}
T=U\begin{pmatrix}T_{1} & 0\\
0 & T_{2}
\end{pmatrix}U,
\end{equation}
and $p\in\mathbb{H}$ with idempotent decomposition as in \eqref{p}.
Furthermore,

\begin{equation}
E_{\mathbb H}T=U\begin{pmatrix}E_{1}T_{1} & 0\\
0 & E_{2}T_{2}
\end{pmatrix}U.
\end{equation}

Assume that $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ are independent with respect to both
$P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$. We have 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}P_{\mathbb{H}}(T\le p) & =U\begin{pmatrix}P_{1}(T_{1}\le\lambda_{1})P_{1}(T_{2}\le\lambda_{2}) & 0\\
0 & P_{2}(T_{1}\le\lambda_{1})P_{2}(T_{2}\le\lambda_{2})
\end{pmatrix}U\\
 & =P_{\mathbb{H}}(T_{1}\le\lambda_{1})P_{\mathbb{H}}(T_{2}\le\lambda_{2}).
\end{split}

\end{equation}

