[Proposition of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] Let the hypotheses of (thm:dragoverkill of the paper) hold for each $\ell$, with respect to $r=r_\ell, \lambda=\lambda_\ell,\delta=\delta_\ell,\sigma=\sigma_\ell$. If moreover, the field satisfies an analogous of the Weyl law, meaning that (eq:pseudoweyl of the paper) holds for some sequence $w_\ell$,
then
\begin{equation}
 \int_{M\times M}\|\sigma_\ell^{-2}j^{1,1}_{x,y}E_\ell\|^4_{\lambda_\ell^2 g} \dd x \dd y\le O(1)r_\ell^{n}
 +w_\ell \delta_\ell^2.
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:sum-prob of arXiv:2608.23554, 'Provably adaptive sampling with uniform and remasking \\ discrete diffusion models'] Consider the uniform or remasking process. For \(i \in [d]\), \(b \in \cV\), \(c\) in the support of \(X_0^i\), and \(t \geq 0\), we have
    \begin{equation}

        \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_0^i = c) = \cF(t, b).
    \end{equation}
    Consequently, for  \(i \in [d]\), \(b \in \cV\), \(x \in \cV^d\), and \(t, u \geq 0\), we have
    \begin{equation}

        \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_{u}^{-i} = x^{-i}) = \cF(t, b),   
    \end{equation} 
    as long as \(\Pr(X_u^{-i} = x^{-i}) > 0\). Furthermore, the same holds for \(\widehat \Pr(\cdot):\)
    \begin{equation*}
        \cF(t, b) = \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \widehat \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_0^i = c) = \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \widehat \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_{u}^{-i} = x^{-i}).
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:superlinear_lyapunov of arXiv:2607.13748, 'Load Balancing with Individually Heterogeneous Servers'] There exists $\alpha, \tilde{C}_1, \tilde{C}_2 \geq 0$ such that for all $\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}_-\times \mathbb{R}_+$, we have 
    \begin{align}
        x_2 \leq \tilde{C}_1 V(\mathbf{x}) + \tilde{C}_2.
    \end{align}

