{"artifact_digest":"sha256:b88068d3159c767083c1e1333964eab8623c09f26b7d3a7302ae90a38d300e00","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/b8/b88068d3159c767083c1e1333964eab8623c09f26b7d3a7302ae90a38d300e00.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:9e8bff32d079c8fb43b30637ca1148d71dbb0e9e48da87cfd29991773d786676","bundle_entry_key":"2606-31455-theorem-16-slln-m","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_061","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_061.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-31455-proposition-16-slln-m","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: \emph{Case 1: $f(0_d)=0$.}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:SLLN-N}, we have
$N(k)/|k|_1\to \mu_\lambda$ almost surely along $k\stackrel{\lambda}{\to}\infty$.
Under $f(0_d)=0$ we have $|X_n|_1\ge 1$ almost surely, hence
$|S_n|_1\ge n$ and therefore $0\le N(k)\le |k|_1$ for all $k$.
Thus $0\le N(k)/|k|_1\le 1$, and the dominated convergence theorem yields

\begin{equation*}
\frac{M(k)}{|k|_1}
=
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac{N(k)}{|k|_1}\right]
\xrightarrow[k\stackrel{\lambda}{\to}\infty]{}
\mu_\lambda.
\end{equation*}

\smallskip
