{"artifact_digest":"sha256:384315322b31dfb08853ecd64b3b282de14fcca15c87e404cf487624ad0dc29f","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/38/384315322b31dfb08853ecd64b3b282de14fcca15c87e404cf487624ad0dc29f.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:cd944ab42b9a4573b0c0d45ec8cedf87692655143130ebdeb3fedc7b1fd817f5","bundle_entry_key":"2506-06559-theorem-23-h-reduction","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_039","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_039.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2506-06559-proposition-23-h-reduction","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We proceed by cases. We first treat the case when
$x$ and $y$ both have degree at least 4:
In this case $E(\tilde{G})=E(G)\cup\{\{x,y\}\}$ and therefore
\[ \prod_{\{z,w\}\in E(G)} \tau(f(z),f(w))\leq \prod_{\{z,w\}\in E(\tilde{G})} \tau(f(z),f(w))\]
simply because $\tau(f(x),f(y))\leq 1$.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\includegraphics[height=.2\textwidth]{Case3.png}
\caption{Diagrammatic estimates for the proof of the Proposition (prop_Hred of the paper) when both $x$ and $y$ have degree larger than $3$.}
\end{figure}
This immediately yields the claim of the lemma.

Now we treat the case when either $x$ or $y$ has degree exactly $3$.
