{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1feb5dba37f99afd533f0eef88ed39d415caae8ced93d05ea6a72e2d22d8127c","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1f/1feb5dba37f99afd533f0eef88ed39d415caae8ced93d05ea6a72e2d22d8127c.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:07802fd3834f765d4471d74162a2bde15ce1c0d54f0537be4420c86008fdb483","bundle_entry_key":"2603-28212-theorem-2-meanvarfn2","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_003","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_003.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-28212-proposition-2-meanvarfn2","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We derive the mean and variance of $F_n^2$ via the first two moments of random vector $(U_n,W_n)$.

Since $\sum_{j\neq i}m_{ij}=m$ for each $i\in[n]$, 
\begin{align}
  \E[U_n] &= \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j\neq i} (m-1+m_{ij}) p\notag\\
          &= \sum_{i=1}^n[(n-1)(m-1)p+mp]\notag\\
          &=(mn-n+1)np.
\end{align}
Noting that there are $3\binom{n}{3}$ possible wedges in $G_{n,p}$ and each of them occurs 
with probability $p^2$, we have
\begin{align}
\E[W_n]=2 \cdot 3\binom{n}{3}p^2=n(n-1)(n-2)p^2.
\end{align}
Taking expectations on both sides of \eqref{sqFn} gives
\begin{align*} 
\E[F_n^2]= m(m+1)n - 2\E[U_n] + \E[W_n].
