body:
[Defin 6 of arXiv:2604.03548, 'Degree-preserving conservative processes and a unified approach for their hydrodynamics'] Let $\rho_0\in L^2(\mathbb T)$. A measurable function $\rho: [0,T] \times \mathbb{T} \to \mathbb R$ is a weak solution to the heat equation with initial profile $\rho_0$
\begin{equation}  

\begin{cases}
\begin{aligned}
& \partial_t \rho(t,u) = D\Delta \rho(t,u)
&& (t,u) \in {(0,T)} \times \mathbb{T},
\\
& \rho(0,u) = \rho_0(u) && u \in \mathbb{T}
\end{aligned}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
if $\rho \in L^2 ([0,T]\times \mathbb T)$ and for all $t \in [0, T]$ and  $H \in C^{1,2}([0, T] \times \mathbb{T}) $ it holds
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb T} \rho(t,u)H(t,u) du
- \int_{\mathbb T} \rho_0(u)H(0,u) du 
- \int_0^t \int_{\mathbb T} \rho(s,u)(\partial_s + \Delta) H(s,u)du ds
= 0. 
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:support-identity of arXiv:2609.02544, 'A finiteness theorem for geodesic Leech wheels'] We have
\begin{equation}
  F(z)^2=2Q(z)+F(z^2)+2C(z)-2.
\end{equation}

[Definition 7 (Smooth Bochner couple) of arXiv:2607.10769, '\fontsize{14}{17}\selectfont\bfseries Sharp Poincar\'e interpolation along Wasserstein geo'] Let $T=\nabla\Phi$ transport $\mu_0$ to $\mu_1$ and set
$H=\Hess{\Phi}$. Given a smooth mean-zero datum $g$ and constants
$a_0,a_1\geq0$, a pair of smooth
vector fields $(X,Y)$ is called a \emph{Bochner couple} with datum $g$
and constants $(a_0,a_1)$ if the relations
\begin{equation}
 Y\circ T=HX,
 \qquad
 -\diver_{\mu_0}X=g,
 \qquad
 -(\diver_{\mu_1}Y)\circ T=g,
\end{equation}
hold, the two matrix symmetry conditions
\begin{equation}
 H DX\ \text{is symmetric},
 \qquad
 ((DY)\circ T)H\ \text{is symmetric}
\end{equation}
hold, and the endpoint Bochner estimates
\begin{equation}
 \norm{X}_{L^2(\mu_0)}\leq a_0\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)},
 \qquad
 \norm{Y}_{L^2(\mu_1)}\leq a_1\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)}
\end{equation}
are valid.  If there is a scalar function $u$ such that
\begin{equation}
 X=H^{-1}\nabla u,
 \qquad
 Y\circ T=\nabla u
\end{equation}
then we say that the couple is generated by the weighted Poisson
equation. Here $H=DT=\Hess{\Phi}$ is the differential of the Brenier map
that relates the two fields.

[Definition wave kernel of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The fundamental solution of the wave equation (or wave kernel) in dimension $d$ is defined by
\begin{align}

p_w(t,x)=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\frac{1}{2}\mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}     & \text{when } d=1, \\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{1}{\sqrt{t^2-|x|^2}} \mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}    & \text{when }d=2,\\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{4\pi t}\sigma_t& \text{when }d=3.
\end{array}\right.
\end{align}
Here $\sigma_t$ is the surface measure on $\partial \mathbf{B}(0,t)$. We notice that for all $d \leq 3$,
\begin{align}

\displaystyle{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}p_w(t,x)\,dx=t}.
\end{align}
For $d \geq 4$, $p_w$ is not a function, but a Schwartz distribution, it will not satisfy one of our assumption, hence the case will not be covered in this dissertation. The Fourier transform of the wave kernel is given by
\begin{align}

    \hat{p}_w(t,\xi)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} e^{-i\xi x}p_w(t,x)dx=\frac{\sin(t|\xi|)}{|\xi|}.
\end{align}

