Scalar factors normalising Gaussian densities are placed in the
differential forms, not in the cycles.

[Problem Problem1 of arXiv:2609.03762, '\vspace*{-2cm} Projected Riemannian Gradient Descent for the Bures--Wasserstein Barycenter'] Let $(R_i)_{i \in [n]} \subseteq \mathrm{PD}(d)$ be a collection of $d \times d$ positive definite matrices and $w := (w_1, \ldots, w_n)$ an associated probability vector.
    Compute the \textit{BW barycenter} of the distribution $(R_i, w_i)_{i \in [n]}$:
    \begin{equation}
        S_\star := \argmin_{S \in \mathrm{PD}(d)} \frac12 \sum_{i=1}^n w_i \mathrm{B}(R_i, S) \equiv \argmin_{S \in \mathrm{PD}(d)} f_1(S).
    \end{equation}
    Throughout, the ensemble is contained in a \textit{spectral interval}: $\alpha I \leq R_i \leq \beta I$ for all ${i \in [n]}$, for some $0 < \alpha \leq \beta < \infty$, and the ratio $\kappa := \beta/\alpha$ is the ensemble's condition number. For a finite ensemble one may take $\alpha = \min_{{i \in [n]}} \lambda_{\min}(R_i)$ and $\beta = \max_{{i \in [n]}} \lambda_{\max}(R_i)$, and the same containment covers distributions of infinite support. (Stated as a problem by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Prop bdy-expect of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1]$. Let $D$ be a bounded, simply connected domain. Fix any $A\subseteq \partial D$ to be a (deterministic) closed subset of the boundary such that $\dim_M(A)<2-\alpha^2/2$. 
    For any continuous, bounded  function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$,
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb E[(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})]=0.
    \end{equation}
    As a consequence,
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})=0,
    \end{equation}
    where the limit is almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.

