[Theorem t:diagram of arXiv:2505.11389, '\bf The Poisson Multiplication Formula'] Consider a vector $(k_1,\dots,k_m)\in \mathbb{N}^m$, as well as symmetric kernels $f_i\in L^2_s (\mu^{k_i})$, $i=1,\dots,m$. If the kernels $f_1,\dots,f_m$ satisfy {\rm \bf Condition A} (see Definition (d:ca of the paper)), then $\Phi := \prod_{i=1}^m I_{k_i}(f_i)\in L^1(\mathbb{P})$, and moreover
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\Phi] = \sum_{\sigma\in \Pi_{\geq 2} (k_1,\dots,k_m) } \int_{\mathcal{Z}^{|\sigma|}}  (f_1\otimes \cdots \otimes f_m)_\sigma \,  d\mu^{|\sigma |},
\end{equation}
where the right-hand side of equation (equation e:diagrams of the paper) equals by definition the product $f_1\cdots f_m \in \mathbb{R} $ in case $k_1=\cdots = k_m = 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 2508070852 of arXiv:2609.02626, 'Comparison principles and symmetry for subquadratic fractional $p$-Laplacian equations'] Let $N\ge 1$, $s\in (0, 1)$, $1<p< 2$, $U\subset \mathbb{R}^N_+$ be a bounded  open set, and $V\in L_{loc}^{1}(U)$ with $V_+(x)=\max\{V(x), 0\}\in L^{\infty}(U)$. Then there exists some $C>0$ depending only on $N, p$ and $s$ such that for any weak solution $u\in \widetilde{W}^{s, p}(U)\cap\widetilde{W}^{s, p}(\mathcal{T}(U))\cap L^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^N)$ of  (equation 2412171452 of the paper) satisfying 
\begin{equation}
|U|^{\frac{sp}{N}}\|V_+\|_{L^{\infty}(U)}\|u\|_{L^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^N)}^{2-p}<C,
\end{equation}
we have $u\ge u^*$ a.e. in $U$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

