[Theorem 46 (Quadratic Mild Supersolutions) of arXiv:2607.03318, 'Continuous Differentiability of the Value Function for Infinite-Dimensional Finite-Horizon'] Let Assumption \ref{ass:coefficients} hold. Assume, moreover, that the unbounded linear operator $A$ satisfies $\langle Ax, x \rangle_H \le \omega \|x\|_H^2$ for some $\omega \in \mathbb{R}$ and all $x \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)$. Let $\phi(x) := \|x\|_H^2$. Then, there exists a constant $C > 0$, such that for any penalization parameter $\varepsilon > 0$ and initial time $t \in [0, T]$, $\phi$ satisfies:
\begin{equation} 

    \phi(x) \ge e^{-\frac{T-t}{\varepsilon}} P_{t,T} [\phi](x) + \int_t^T e^{-\frac{s-t}{\varepsilon}} P_{t,s} \left[ \frac{1}{\varepsilon} \phi - C(1+\phi) \right](x) \, \mathrm{d}s.
\end{equation}

