body:
[Lemma L:collection_RG_facts of arXiv:2512.21977, 'Repeat times and a two-weight UST model'] Consider the critical Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi  random graph $\mathbb{G}(n,p)$ with $p=p(n)=1/n$. The following facts hold true.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item  There exists a constant $c > 0$ such that, if $1\leq j \leq  n^{1/5}$, then 
        \begin{equation}
            \mathbb{P} \big( |\mathcal{C}(1)|=j \big) \geq \frac{c}{j^{3/2}}.
        \end{equation}

        \item  Suppose that $1\leq j\leq n^{1/5}$. Then
        \begin{equation}
            \mathbb{P} \big( \mathcal{C}(1) \text{ contains a cycle} \ \big| \ |\mathcal{C}(1)|=j \big )\ll 1 .
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma mshe of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp With notation to be explained afterwards, we have the following \abbr{SPDE}:

\begin{align}
\d\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\x}&=\mathscr{T}_{N}\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\x}\d\t+\sqrt{2}\lambda N^{\frac12}\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\x}\d\mathbf{b}_{\t,\x}+\mathfrak{e}[\tau_{\x}{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{\t}]\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\x}\d\t.
\end{align}

Above, the Brownian motions $\mathbf{b}$ are exactly the ones from (equation eq:curr of the paper). Also, {\small$\mathscr{T}_{N}:=N^{2}\alpha\Delta+\frac14N\lambda^{2}\Delta$} as in Proposition (prop:s-sde of the paper), and $\alpha,\lambda$ are defined after (equation eq:ch of the paper). Finally, the error term $\mathfrak{e}$ is given in (equation eq:msheII of the paper).

[Lemma 19 (Passive Nash smoothing) of arXiv:2608.30715, 'Endpoint Energy Atoms Force Local Pressure Concentration in Three-Dimensional Navier--Stok'] Let \(z=S(t,\tau)h\) solve \eqref{eq:passive}. For
\(1\le p\le\infty\),
\begin{equation}
 \norm{z(t)}_p\le\norm{h}_p

\end{equation}
whenever the right-hand side is finite. In addition,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{S(t,\tau)h}_\infty
 \le C_\Omega\bigl(1+[\nu(t-\tau)]^{-3/4}\bigr)\norm{h}_2,
 \qquad t>\tau.

\end{equation}

