body:
[Prop of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The diagram
    \[
        \begin{tikzcd}
            \cat{$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_{/\textbf{\textup{Gr}}}\arrow[d," \cat{$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_{/i}"']\arrow[r,"\textbf{\textup{th}}"] & \cat{$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*\arrow[d,"\Sigma^\bullet"]\\
            \cat{$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_{/\textbf{\textup{BOP}}}\arrow[r,"\textbf{\textup{Th}}"']&\textbf{\textup{Sp}}
        \end{tikzcd}
    \]
    of symmetric monoidal (topological) functors commutes up to symmetric monoidal isomorphism.
    \begin{proof}
        As before, it suffices to prove this after restriction to $(\textbf{\textup{y}}_{/\textbf{\textup{Gr}}})^{\textup{op}}$.

        By definition, the bottom composite becomes $\textbf{\textup{f}}\circ\textbf{\textup{y}}_{/i}^{\textup{op}}$. Recall that $i\colon\textbf{\textup{Gr}}\to\textbf{\textup{BOP}}$ was defined in degree $V$ by $U\mapsto V\oplus U$, so that this composite is given on objects by sending $w\colon\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)\to\textbf{\textup{Gr}}$ with $W\coloneqq w(\textup{id}_V)$ to $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp S^{V\oplus W}$. We have an isomorphism $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp S^V\cong\Sigma^\bullet\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)_+$ given in degree $U\in\textbf{\textup{O}}$ by sending $(i\colon V\to U,u\in\im(i)^\perp)\smashp x$ to $(i(x)+u)\smashp i$, which then induces an isomorphism $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp S^{V\oplus W}\cong \Sigma^\bullet(\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)_+\smashp S^W)$. We omit the straightforward but lengthy computation this is natural and compatible with the symmetric monoidal structures and hence identifies $\textbf{\textup{f}}\circ\textbf{\textup{y}}_{/i}$ with the description of $\Sigma^\bullet\circ\textbf{\textup{th}}|_{(\textbf{\textup{y}}_{/\textbf{\textup{Gr}}})}$ from Proposition~(prop:th-on-corep-sym-mon of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 7 of arXiv:2608.29406, 'Partially Totally Real Submanifolds of Sasakian Manifolds'] A PTR-submanifold is said to have a \emph{maximal totally real distribution} if
\begin{equation}
 \Dperp=\ker(P|_{TM\cap\ker\eta}).
\end{equation}

