[Definition 95 (Bottleneck ratio) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] The bottleneck ratio of $(G,\weight)$ is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
          \Phi_{(G, \weight)} := \min_{0 < \pi(S) \leq \frac{1}{2}}\Phi_{(G, \weight)}(S).
    \end{equation*}

[Thm 1 of arXiv:2511.02763, 'The Cayley-Moser problem with Poissonian arrival of offers'] The optimal policy $\mu(t)$ is given by the solution of the initial value problem
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{cases}
			\mu'(t)=\lambda \phi(\mu(t))\\
			\mu(0)=\mu_0,
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	where $\phi$ is defined by \eqref{defphi}.
	\eqref{ode} can be integrated to obtain the 
	explicit expression
	\begin{equation}\mu(t)=\Psi^{-1}(\lambda t),\end{equation}
	where $\Psi^{-1}:[0,\infty)\rightarrow [\mu_0,M)$ is the inverse of the function
	\begin{equation}\Psi(x)\doteq\int_{\mu_0}^x \frac{du}{\phi(u)},\;\;\;\; x\in [\mu_0,M).\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lemma4.6 of arXiv:2609.02607, 'Spatial inhomogeneity for a three-dimensional doubly degenerate nutrient system with indir'] Let $K>0$ with the property that (equation 1.6 of the paper) holds.  Then  we have
\begin{align}
    v(\cdot,t)\rightarrow 0 ~~\mbox{in}~~W^{1,p}(\Omega)~~\mbox{for all}~~p\geq 1\qquad \mbox{as}~~ t\to \infty.
\end{align}

