[Lemma lem:vd-l2-polygonal-poisson of arXiv:2609.00698, 'Kinetic Wedge Layers and Diffusive Limit of Neutron Transport in Polygonal Domains'] Let $\varrho\in L^2(\Omega)$, and let $\phi\in H^1_0(\Omega)$ be the unique weak solution of the Poisson problem
\begin{align}
 &-\Delta\phi=\varrho
 \quad\hbox{in }\Omega.

\end{align}
For each reentrant vertex $V_m$, recall from (equation eq:intro-zero-ray-pairs-comp of the paper) that $\lambda_{m,1}=\pi/\omega_m\in(\frac12,1)$. There are fixed cutoffs $\chi_m^{\rm D}\in C_c^\infty(B_{2\ell_{\mathrm{vtx}}}(V_m))$, equal to one in $B_{\ell_{\mathrm{vtx}}}(V_m)$ and supported in pairwise disjoint vertex neighborhoods so small that $\operatorname{supp}\chi_m^{\rm D}$ meets only the two side collars incident to $V_m$, and coefficients $c_m\in\mathbb R$, such that $\phi$ has the singular decomposition
\begin{align}
 &\phi
 =
 \phi_{\rm reg}
 +\sum_{m:\,\omega_m>\pi}
 c_m\chi_m^{\rm D}
 r_{m}^{\lambda_{m,1}}
 \sin(\lambda_{m,1}\theta_m),
 \qquad
 \phi_{\rm reg}\in H^2(\Omega).

\end{align}
With $\nm{\nabla_x\phi}_{L^2_{\rm pw}(\partial\Omega)}^2:=\sum_j\nm{\nabla_x\phi\big|_{E_j}}_{L^2(E_j)}^2$, its parts are controlled by the data:
\begin{align}
 &\nm{\phi_{\rm reg}}_{H^2(\Omega)}
 +\sum_{m:\,\omega_m>\pi}\abs{c_m}
 +\nm{\nabla_x\phi}_{L^2_{\rm pw}(\partial\Omega)}
 \leq C_\Omega\nm{\varrho}_{L^2(\Omega)}.

\end{align}
Moreover the range of exponents
\begin{align}
 &\frac43<p_{\rm P}\leq2,
 \qquad
 p_{\rm P}<\frac{2}{2-\lambda_{m,1}}
 \quad\hbox{for every }m\hbox{ with }\omega_m>\pi,
 \qquad
 p_{\rm P}':=\frac{p_{\rm P}}{p_{\rm P}-1},

\end{align}
is nonempty, and for every $p_{\rm P}$ in it the Hessian obeys
\begin{align}
 &\nm{D_x^2\phi}_{L^{p_{\rm P}}(\Omega)}
 \leq C_{\Omega,p_{\rm P}}\nm{\varrho}_{L^2(\Omega)},

\end{align}
with the conjugate-exponent gain
\begin{align}
 &\frac{2}{p_{\rm P}'}=2-\frac{2}{p_{\rm P}}
 >\frac12.

\end{align}

