[Lemma per2.lem of arXiv:2604.24389, '``True" self-avoiding walks on general trees'] We have
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb{P}}(\tau^+_{\rho} = \infty) = {\mathbb{P}} (|\mathcal{C}(\rho)| = \infty) = {\mathbb{P}}(|\mathcal{C}_{\rm CP}(\rho)| = \infty).
\end{align*} 
Consequently, the process $\mathbf{X }$ \begin{itemize}
    \item is a.s. recurrent if $ \mathbb{P}(|\mathcal{C}_{\rm CP}(\rho)| < \infty)=1$, 
\item is a.s. transient if $\mathbb{P}(|\mathcal{C}_{\rm CP}(\rho)| = \infty)>0$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

