body:
[Theorem Locality of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{thm:lrp_locality}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 2$, and suppose that $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ is a transitive kernel satisfying $J(x,y) = \Omega(d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha \in (0, 2)$. 

    Let $(G_n,J_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ be a sequence of transitive graphs and kernels converging locally to $(G,J)$ with $\liminf_{n \to \infty} \dim(G_n) \geq 2$.

    If $\alpha \leq 1$, or if $\alpha > 1$ and $(J_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ is uniformly integrable, then 

    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n \to \infty}
        \beta_c(G_n,J_n) 
        =
        \beta_c(G,J).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 13 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] The pair $(f_q^\mu,C_q^\mu)$ converges to $(f^\mu,C^\mu)$ as $q\to 0+$, that is, 
\begin{align}
   f_q^\mu\to f^\mu\ \text{in}\ C(K),\qquad C_q^\mu\to C^\mu.
 \end{align}

