body:
[Definition 4 of arXiv:2605.31344, 'Subcritical sharpness for real-valued spin models'] Let $\beta$ and $\rho$ be fixed.
    An infinite-volume Gibbs measure is a probability measure $\nu$ on $\mathbb{R}^{V}$, with the $\sigma-$algebra generated by Borel events depending on finitely many vertices, such that for any finite $\Lambda \subset V$ and any bounded measurable function $g: \mathbb{R}^{\Lambda} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, the DLR equation holds:
    \begin{equation*}
        \nu[g] = \int_{\eta \in \mathbb{R}^{V}} \langle g\rangle _{\Lambda, \beta, \rho}^{\eta} \mathrm{d} \nu(\eta).
    \end{equation*}

[Lemma lemma of weakly 2 of arXiv:2605.20800, 'On the maximal displacement of subcritical branching random walk in random environment'] For any $\delta\in (0,\rho)$, there exists some constant $ C_\delta <\infty $, such that for any $x\geqslant1$, we have
		\begin{equation*}
			Q^\rho_E\otimes P_{\overline{Y}} \left(
			e^{(1-\delta)R_x} B_x
			\right) \leqslant C_\delta.
		\end{equation*}
	Then by the H\"older inequality, we have
	\begin{equation}
		Q^\rho_E\otimes P_{\overline{Y}} \left(
		e^{(1-\delta)R_x} B_x
		\right) \leqslant C_\delta^{\frac{1-\rho}{1-\delta}}<\infty.
	\end{equation}

