[Proposition prop:hyp-expo of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Let $ \mathsf{S}$ the spectral measure of a $ L^{ 2}_{ loc}$ weakly stationary random measure~ $ \mathsf{M}.$
Assume $ \mathsf{S}$ admits exponent $ \alpha>0 $. Let $ f$ an $ \mathsf{S}-$admissible function  {of $   \mathscr  F_{ \gamma }^{ *}(\mathbb{R}^{ d})$. Then} 
\begin{align*}
 \textrm{Var}\left(\mathsf{M}(f_{R})\right) =   
 \begin{cases} 
 O(R^{d-\min(\alpha ,\gamma )  })$  id $\alpha \neq \gamma  .\\
 O(R^{ d-\alpha }\ln(R)))$  if $\alpha  =  \gamma . 
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
In this case we say that $ \mathsf{M}$ is  {\it  $ \alpha $-hyperuniform.}

If conversely $  \textrm{Var}\left(\mathsf{P}(f_{ R})\right) = O(R^{ d-\alpha })$ holds for some $ \mathsf{S}$-admissible function $ f$ with $ \int_{}f\neq 0$, then $ \mathsf{S}$ admits exponent $ \alpha $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

