[Definition 49 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Potential functions $C^0(M)$]
Given $M \subset M_0$. $C^0(M)$ is the collection of all $\mathbf{Z}$-valued linear functions defined on $C_0(M)$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
C^0(M):=Hom(C_0(M), \mathbf{Z}),
\end{equation*}
where $C_0(M)$ is the collection of all formal finite sums of the vertices of $M$ i.e.,
\begin{multline*}
C_0(M):=\{ n_1v_1 + n_2v_2 + \ldots + n_kv_k  \ |\   k \in \mathbf{N},\\ 
n_i  \in \mathbf{Z},\  v_i \in Ver(M) \ (i=1, 2, \ldots, k))\}.
\end{multline*}
Elements of $C^0(M)$ are called \textit{potential functions} (or \textit{$0$-forms}) on $M$.

[Theorem 16 (Component density in the BJR model) of arXiv:2607.16833, 'Dynamical Equations for Poisson Galton--Watson Trees and Component Densities of Sparse Inh'] Under \eqref{eq:vertex-space}, \eqref{eq:BJR-edge-prob}, and conditions
\textup{(G1)}--\textup{(G3)},
\begin{equation}
 \frac{K(G_n)}n\longrightarrow\gamma(u)
 =\bE_\pi\left[\frac1{T_u}\right]
 \qquad\text{in probability and in $L^1$.}
\end{equation}
Consequently, $\bE K(G_n)/n\to\gamma(u)$.

