	\end{equation} 

	For each inner factor we restrict $q_i$ and $q_j$ to occur exactly once in the $q_{z_i}$, $u_i, v_i$, and $q_i$ and $q_j$ cannot occur together in a $(u_i, v_i)$-pairs unless $n_1^{(\mathrm{off})} = 1$ and $n_1^{(\mathrm{diag})} = 0$. Also, if we have equality in (equation no_empty_inner_fac of the paper) and $n_1^{(\mathrm{inner})} = 2$ we require $y_1 \geq 1$. Additionally, we restrict each index $i_j \in \mathcal{I}$ to occur exactly twice in the indices $x_i, u_i, v_i, w_i, \hat{u}_j, \hat{v}_j, \hat{w}_j$ among all inner factors. We say that the indices $i_1, \dots, i_{\mathfrak{m}_0}$ have inhomogeneous weights $\prod_{p=1}^{\mathfrak{m}_0} (\mathring{V}^{k_p})_{i_p i_{p+1}}$, and that the remaining indices have homogeneous weights $1/N$. The indices $i_1$ and $i_{\mathfrak{m}_0}$ are special, since for these indices $\sum_{i_1} \prod_{p=1}^{\mathfrak{m}_0 - 1} (\mathring{V}^{k_p})_{i_p i_{p+1}} = \sum_{i_{\mathfrak{m}_0}} \prod_{p=1}^{\mathfrak{m}_0 - 1} (\mathring{V}^{k_p})_{i_p i_

