{"artifact_digest":"sha256:ef3d881cb793a0ffe9de99bcbd42e535bc1b5084ee0ee22fdfbffb87ea6b3aa1","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ef/ef3d881cb793a0ffe9de99bcbd42e535bc1b5084ee0ee22fdfbffb87ea6b3aa1.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a6b6f99dd48f19ddaaff61ac868d67b2f13b45fd9a2471eb6be8cec3801105f5","bundle_entry_key":"2608-24863-theorem-20-permutation-invariant-poincar-","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_107","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_107.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-24863-proposition-20-permutation-invariant-poincar","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Subtract the mean and set $h=Q_{N,\beta}f$, $u=f-h$.  Then, $h$ is symmetric and entire, while $u\perp\mathcal A^2_{N,\beta}$.  Since constants lie in $\mathcal A^2_{N,\beta}$, $h$ has mean zero; \Cref{lem:phase} with $G=1$ gives $h(0)=0$.

Set $k=Q_{N,\beta}\bar h$.  The projection commutes with permutation averaging, so $k$ is permutation-invariant.  \Cref{lem:pole-removal} makes $k$ entire.  Projection orthogonality gives
\begin{equation}
 \|k\|_2^2=\int_{\C^N} \bar h\,\bar k\,\D \mathbb P_{N,\beta}
 =\overline{\int_{\C^N} hk\,\D \mathbb P_{N,\beta}}
 =\overline{h(0)k(0)}=0.
