{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7929182dbc46f246effd58014c97b6c247b3f888942ca3960100869a462f8751","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/79/7929182dbc46f246effd58014c97b6c247b3f888942ca3960100869a462f8751.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b532e2bbf2b2f67db6368ab06758d6330c929232a22b31dabc29807738896d7e","bundle_entry_key":"2411-09949-theorem-15-sy","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_099","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_099.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-09949-proposition-15-sy","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: (i) Since $\mu$ is the stationary distribution of SDE (equation ou of the paper), we have for any $f\in{\mathbf C}^2$,
$$
\int_{\mathbb R} \big(\Delta^{\frac\alpha 2}f(x)-x f'(x)\big)\mu({\mathord{{\rm d}}} x)=0.
$$
In particular, taking $f(x)=\mathrm{e}^{-i zx}$, we get
\begin{align*}
\int_{\mathbb R} \Delta^{\frac\alpha 2}\mathrm{e}^{-i zx}\mu({\mathord{{\rm d}}} x)=-i z\int_{\mathbb R} x\mathrm{e}^{-i zx}\mu({\mathord{{\rm d}}} x)=z\partial_z\int_{\mathbb R}  \mathrm{e}^{-i zx}\mu({\mathord{{\rm d}}} x).
