[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2605.06953, 'Asymptotic Results for Uniform Group Drawing in the Coupon Collector's Problem'] If $s \ge 2$ is a constant, then
     \begin{align*}
         \E[Y] = \frac{n}{s}\cdot H_{n} -\frac{s-1}{2s}\cdot H_{n} + O\left(1\right),
     \end{align*}
      as $n\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm gform of arXiv:2604.24061, 'A characterization of ruin-inducing probability measures in a renewal risk model'] The following assertions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
for every  \(Q{\,\in\,}\hyperlink{rms}{\mathcal{R}_S}\) there exists an essentially unique  \((\gamma,\delta){\,\in\,}\hyperlink{rp}{\mathcal C_P}\) satisfying conditions (equation mart of the paper) and (equation mart1 of the paper). In particular, the ruin probability admits the representation
\begin{gather}
\psi(u)=\mathbb E_Q\Big[e^{-S_{\tau_u}^{(\gamma)}-T^{(\delta)}_{N_{\tau_u}}}\Big]\,\,\text{ for all } u\in\mathbb R_+;

\tag{$\text{ruin}(P)$}
\end{gather} 
\item
conversely, for every \((\gamma,\delta){\,\in\,}\hyperlink{rp}{\mathcal C_P}\), condition (equation mq of the paper) determines a unique probability measure $Q{\,:=\,}Q^{(\gamma,\delta)}{\,\in\,}\hyperlink{rms}{\mathcal{R}_S}$ satisfying  (equation mart1 of the paper). In particular, the ruin probability admits the representation (equation ruin of the paper).
\end{enumerate}

