{"artifact_digest":"sha256:347173c6201cb02567f6cca6e2b1d3385d6bfa982aeeee8b5ad4051e4ac28881","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/34/347173c6201cb02567f6cca6e2b1d3385d6bfa982aeeee8b5ad4051e4ac28881.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:96f680fcc456c42cb1d3b4d9af83f996ee647c549316d908521c45ebf361d140","bundle_entry_key":"2609-01362-lemma-16-keylemma","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_141","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_141.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-01362-lemma-16-keylemma","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Fix $1 \leq i < j < k < l \leq n$ as in the lemma. 
    Suppose there exists a pair of paths $\gamma, \tilde \gamma$ connecting $v_i$ to $v_k$ and $v_j$ to $v_l$ respectively, which do not intersect at a vertex of $V_{\mathrm{Ising}}$. 
    We claim that in this case
    \begin{equation}
	    \E [ \vp^+_{v_i}\vp^+_{v_k} \vp^-_{v_j} \vp^-_{v_l} ] > 0, 
    \end{equation}
