[Theorem thm:Gaussian:moments of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] Let \( \gamma \) be a Radon Gaussian measure on \( (X,\mathcal{B}(X)) \).
    Then
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exists a unique element \( a \in X \) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            f(a) = \int_X f(x) \, \gamma(dx), \qquad \forall f \in X^*.
        \end{equation*}

        \item There exists a unique linear operator \( R : X^* \to X \) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \langle Rf,g \rangle =
            \int_X (f(x)-a_\gamma(f))(g(x)-a_\gamma(g)) \, \gamma(dx)
            \qquad \forall f, g \in X^*
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:pure of arXiv:2607.19844, 'Binomial probabilities at a fixed distance from the mode:\\ size-biasing and the complete '] Under the hypotheses of Theorem~(thm:old of the paper),
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{gathered}
		b(m;N,p)\sim\frac{Np}{m}\cdot\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi Npq}}\;
		\exp\Bigl(\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{\widetilde A_k(t;p)}{N^{k}}\Bigr),
		\\
		\widetilde A_k(t;p)=\frac{(-1)^{k+1}B_{k+1}-\Xi_k(p)\,B_{k+1}(t)}{k(k+1)} ,
	\end{gathered}
	\end{equation}
	with the same uniformity and the same remainder estimate: for every $M$, truncating the
	exponent after $M$ terms leaves a relative error $O(N^{-M-1})$, uniformly on $K\times H$.  The
	coefficients $\widetilde A_k$ contain nothing but Bernoulli numbers and Bernoulli polynomials: no power
	of $t$ stands outside a $B_{k+1}(t)$.

[Definition Def_SliceDerivative of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] The \defined{slice distribution} $\mathscr{F}^\mathbf{Sl} \subseteq T\paren{\mathbf{Dir}\times\mathbf{Ram}}$ is the distribution induced by the slices foliating $\mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$;
  that is:
  for every $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$
  \begin{equation*}    
    \mathscr{F}_{{\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]}^\mathbf{Sl} \coloneq T_{{\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]}\mathbf{Sl}_{{\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]}
  \end{equation*}
  with $\mathbf{Sl}_{{\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]}$ denoting the unique slice through $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}])$.
  The canonical trivialisations of $\mathbf{E}|_\mathbf{Sl}$, $\mathbf{F}|_\mathbf{Sl}$, and $\mathbf{G}|_\mathbf{Sl}$ defined by (Prop_Universal_Slice of the paper) define the \defined{slice partial covariant derivatives} $\nabla^\mathbf{Sl}$ with respect to $\mathscr{F}^\mathbf{Sl}$ on $\mathbf{E}$, $\mathbf{F}$, and $\mathbf{G}$.

