[Definition def:Qleqr of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] If $Q$ is a quadratic form on $\mathbb{R}^m$ and $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$, then we write
  \begin{equation}
    Q\leq \lambda \quad \iff \quad Q(x)\leq \lambda \|x\|^2,\, \forall x\in \mathbb{R}^m\setminus \{0\}
  \end{equation}
  and similarly for $>,\leq$ and $<$. In particular $Q>0$ means that $Q$ is positive definite.

[Lem 7 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] The function $\varphi_q^\mu(x)$ is continuous on $\R$. Moreover, there exists a constant $C_q^\mu$ such that $\varphi_q^\mu|_K$ satisfies the following equation:
\begin{align}

(I-T_q^\mu)(\varphi_q^\mu|_K)=C_q^\mu,\qquad \mu[\varphi_q^\mu|_K]+\frac{C_q^\mu}{r_q(0)}=1.
\end{align}

