[Definition 3 of arXiv:2606.06757, 'Path-Dependent Ergodic Optimal Control \\ and Backward Stochastic Differential Equations'] We say that a family of measures $(m_t)_{t \in \R}$ on $\C_{\theta}$ is an \textnormal{evolution system of measures} for the transition semi-group $P_{s,t}$ if $m_s P_{s, t} = m_t$, \textnormal{i.e.} 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{array}{cc}
             \displaystyle \int_{\mathcal{C}_{\theta}} P_{s, t} [\phi](\xi) \, m_s(d\xi) = \int_{\mathcal{C}_{\theta}} \phi(\xi) \, m_t(d\xi) \, , & \text{for all } \, s \leq t \in \mathbb{R}~\mbox{and}~\phi~\mbox{bounded continuous}.
        \end{array}
    \end{equation*}

[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2608.13235, 'Mixing Properties of Random Laguerre Tessellations'] Let $\ppp$ be a marked Poisson point process on $\rd \times \R$ with intensity measure $\gamma \lambda^d \otimes \markdist$, where $\gamma > 0$  and $\markdist$ is a probability measure on $\R$. Then $L(\ppp)$ is \as a tessellation of $\rd$ if and only if the typical mark $M \sim \markdist$ satisfies 
    \begin{equation} 
    \e M_-^\frac{d}{2}< \infty.
\end{equation}

