[Theorem t:control_mesoscopic_energy of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] Suppose that Assumption (ass:forcing_regularity_iteration of the paper) holds. Let $\mu>0$, $r\in (1,\infty)$ and $p_0\in (2,p)$.
Then there exists a constant $R_0>0$ depending only on $\mu,p,q,r,p_0$ and $d$ such that the following assertion holds. For all local solutions $(v^N,\pi^N,(\widetilde{\pi}^N_{k,\alpha})_{k,\alpha})$ to the stochastic Stokes system (equation eq:turbulent_Stokes_scaling_original_iteration of the paper) on $Q$, the following uniform control of the energy at the microscopic scales holds
\begin{align*}
\big({\mathbf E} \|v^N \|_{\underline{L}^2(N^{-1}Q)}^r\big)^{1/r}
&\leq  R_0\Big({\mathbf E}\sup_{I} \|v^{N}\|_{L^2(B)}^{r}\Big)^{1/r}+R_0 \mathcal{L}_{Q},
\end{align*}
as well as a uniform control of the oscillation of the deterministic and stochastic pressures at the microscopic scales
\begin{align*}
\Big({\mathbf E} \Big\|\pi^N-\fint_{N^{-1}B}\pi^N \Big\|_{\underline{L}^2(N^{-1}I;\underline{H}^{-1}(N^{-1} B))}^r\Big)^{1/r}
&\leq R_0 \mathcal{L}_{Q},\\
\Big({\mathbf E} \Big\|\Big( \widetilde{\pi}_{k,\alpha}^N-\fint_{N^{-1}B}\widetilde{\pi}_{k,\alpha}^N\Big)_{k,\alpha}\Big\|_{\underline{L}^{p_0}(N^{-1}I;\underline{L}^2(N^{-1}B;\ell^2))}^r\Big)^{1/r}
&\leq R_0 \mathcal{L}_{Q},
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{L}_{Q}$ is the $L^p(L^q)$-content of $(v^N,\pi^N,(\widetilde{\pi}^N_{k,\alpha})_{k,\alpha})$ on the (large) scale $Q$, i.e.,
\begin{align}
\nonumber
\mathcal{L}_{Q}
=\big( \max\big\{{\mathbf E} \|F \|_{L^p(I;L^q(B))}^r,\,
{\mathbf E} \|G \|_{L^p(I;L^q(B))}^r , \,
{\mathbf E} \|v^N \|_{L^2(Q)}^r
,&\\

{\mathbf E} \|\nabla \pi^N\|_{L^2(I;H^{-1}(B))}^r,\, 
{\mathbf E} \|( \nabla \widetilde{\pi}_{k,\alpha}^N )_{k,\alpha}\|_{L^{p_0}(I;H^{-1}(B;\ell^2))}^r &\,\big\}\big)^{1/r}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2606.13648, 'Stochastic dominations for FK percolation and sharp thinning thresholds for the Ising ener'] Let $A$ be an event that does not depend on edge $e$, let $p\in(0,1]^E$ be a parameter, and let $\omega\sim \en_{J,p}$. Then, 
\begin{equation}
\partial_{J_e}[\en_{J,p}(A)]= \frac{2}{p_e}\cov_{J,p}(\1_A,\omega_e),
\end{equation}
where covariance is taken with respect to the measure $\en_{J,p}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 16 (Shifted Integral Operator) of arXiv:2607.03318, 'Continuous Differentiability of the Value Function for Infinite-Dimensional Finite-Horizon'] Let $\varepsilon > 0$ and consider a shift parameter $L \ge 0$. The \emph{nonlinear integral operator} $\mathcal{M}_{\varepsilon, L} : \mathcal{B}_m([0, T] \times H) \to \mathcal{B}_m([0, T] \times H)$ is defined by:
\begin{equation} 

    \mathcal{M}_{\varepsilon, L}(u)(t, x) := e^{-L(T-t)} P_{t,T}[\Psi](x) + \int_t^T e^{-L(s-t)} P_{t,s} \left[ f(s, \cdot) + L u(s, \cdot) - \frac{1}{\varepsilon} \left( u(s, \cdot) - \Phi(s, \cdot) \right)^+ \right](x) \, \mathrm{d}s.
\end{equation}

