{"artifact_digest":"sha256:8c3c6d0dadedb0c0702ac0b4b13c4b5947db52ff27119da76976033677f6b986","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/8c/8c3c6d0dadedb0c0702ac0b4b13c4b5947db52ff27119da76976033677f6b986.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6479ee448bcdabc2742df4bc89848f35edc2885deed906f4695c60eddf861bd6","bundle_entry_key":"2509-01797-theorem-69-prop-exp-beta-gamma","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_007","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s0_007.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2509-01797-prop-69-prop-exp-beta-gamma","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $y>0$. 
By \eqref{Eq A E N},
\begin{equation}

\ind_{\Ns_{e^{-4\pi y/\gamma^{2}}}(A)}
= 
\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
\sum_{j=0}^{N} (-1)^{j}
\dfrac{\gamma^{2j+1}}{2^{2j+1/2} j! (j+1/2)}
\dfrac{\psi_{2j+1,A}}{y^{j + 1/2}}
+
o(\gamma^{2N+1}),
\end{equation}
where $o(\gamma^{2N+1})$ is in the $L^{2}(d\PP,\sigma(\Phi),H^{-\eta}(\C))$ sense.
The idea now is to integrate the above expansion for $y\in (0,+\infty)$
against
\begin{displaymath}
\Big(\dfrac{\beta}{y}-1\Big)
y^{\beta} e^{-y}\, dy,
\end{displaymath}
as in Lemma \ref{Lem beta gamma int}.
The condition $\beta>N + 1/2$ ensures the integrability of all the terms of the expansion.
To be more precise, we will split the integral into two ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:5d2197d8955f7b237e1374641bf772fdbc98c90e1e40609c85e783f31d22cf02","revision_digest":"sha256:4cd3387f844e3ea15ea23c527b49f7580e8256f6431555eeb0c18aabea464f23","source_id":"arxiv:2509.01797","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

