[Theorem weakconvgapsthm of arXiv:2608.04730, 'A new look at some aspects of one-dimensional random sequential adsorption and its continu'] Consider the probability  measures $\{\mu^{\text{gaps}}_k\}_{k=2}^\infty$ defined in (equation muk of the paper), where $g_{k;l}$ is as in (equation lgaps of the paper) and $m_k$ is as in (equation mk of the paper). Then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{k\to\infty}\mu^{\text{gaps}}_k([0,\gamma])=\frac2{m_\infty}\int_0^\infty\left(1-e^{-\gamma x}\right)\exp\left(-2\int_0^x\frac{1-e^{-y}}ydy\right)dx, \ \gamma\in[0,1],
\end{equation}
where $m_\infty$ is the R\'enyi parking constant from (equation minfty of the paper). In particular, the limiting probability measure
\begin{equation}
\mu^{\text{gaps}}_\infty:=\text{w}-\lim_{k\to\infty}\mu^{\text{gaps}}_k
\end{equation}
has density
\begin{equation}
f_{\mu^{\text{gaps}}_\infty}(\gamma)=\frac2{m_\infty}\int_0^\infty xe^{-\gamma x}\exp\left(-2\int_0^x\frac{1-e^{-y}}ydy\right)dx, \ \gamma\in[0,1].
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition GrowthBoundCond of arXiv:2411.12522, 'Canonical insurance models: stochastic equations and comparison theorems'] Let $\mathbb{Y}^+$ denote the set of all $\mathcal{F}$-adapted, nonnegative, nondecreasing, univariate  càdlàg processes
	$Y$ for which there exists a growth bound  
	\begin{align}
		Y(t) \leq g(t) \Big( 1+ \sum_{i,j:i \neq j} N^{ij}(t )\Big)^n, \quad t \geq 0,
	\end{align}
	for some function  $g:[0, \infty) \rightarrow [0,\infty)$ and a positive integer $n \in \mathbb{N}$; both $g$ and $n$ may depend on $Y$. By $\mathbb{Y}$ we denote the set of all processes that can be generated as a difference of processes from $\mathbb{Y}^+$.

[Defi Pnk and its convolution of arXiv:2604.23890, 'The Cutoff Profile for Random Transpositions on Repeated Cards in the Full Range of Parame'] Let $P_n$ be the random permutation given by
\begin{equation}
P_n:=\begin{cases}
(i j) & \text{with probability }\frac{2}{n^2}\\
\Id & \text{with probability }\frac{1}{n}\end{cases}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for any $t\ge 1$, let $P_{n}^{*t}$ be the $t$-fold convolution of $P_n$, viewed $P_{n}^{*t}$ as a random variable in $\mathfrak{S}_n$. Given $n=ml$ with $l\ge 2$, the random variable $\sim_l\backslash P_{n}^{*t}\in\sim_l\backslash\mathfrak{S}_n$ is induced from (defi: quotient of space of the paper) in the obvious way.

[Lemma 67 (Finite logarithmic addition) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] For \(r\geq1\), \(m\geq2\), and compact operators \(T_1,\ldots,T_m\),

\begin{equation}
 \left\|\sum_{j=1}^mT_j\right\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r]}}
 \leq C_r(1+\log m)^r
 \sum_{j=1}^m\|T_j\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r]}}.

\end{equation}

The same estimate, with a constant also depending on the fixed tensor
degree, holds in every multi-flattening space.

[Theorem th9 of arXiv:2411.17104, 'Two-barriers-reflected BSDE with Rank-based Data'] Assume that (\textbf{A1)}-(\textbf{A3)} are satisfied. If for each positive $R$, there exists a positive function $\zeta_R$ on $[0,\infty)$ with
$\lim_{r\rightarrow 0^+} \zeta_R (r) =0$
such that
\begin{equation}
\big|G(t,x_{1},y,z)-G(t,x_{2},y,z)\big|\leq\zeta_R\left((1+|z|)|x_{1}-x_{2}|\right),
\end{equation}
for all $t\in [0,T]$,\;$x_{1},x_{2}\in \Gamma^{n}$,\;$y\in \mathbb{R}$,\;$z\in \mathbb{R}^n$ satisfying $max\big\{|x_{1}|,|x_{2}|,|y|\big\} \leq R$.
Then (equation 4 of the paper) admits at most one viscosity solution such that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{|x|\rightarrow\infty}|u(t,x)|e^{-A\log^2|x|}=0,\text{ uniformly in }t\in[0,T],
\end{equation}
for some $A>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem l:Yproperty of arXiv:2411.10586, 'A convergence framework for Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble\\ via pole evolution'] For any particle-generated Nevanlinna function $Y:\mathbb{H}\to\mathbb{H}\cup{\mathbb R}$, the quantity ${\rm Im}[w]{\rm Im}[Y(w)]$ is monotone in ${\rm Im}[w]$; the derivatives of $Y$ satisfy
\begin{align}
|\del^k_w Y(w)|\leq \frac{k!{\rm Im}[Y(w)]}{{\rm Im}[w]^k}\leq \frac{k!|Y(w)|}{{\rm Im}[w]^k},
\end{align}
where $\del^k_wY (w)$ is the $k$-th derivative of $Y$, for any integer $k\ge 2$.

