body:
[Thm 3 of arXiv:2607.17576, 'How much randomness is needed to symmetrize a random variable?'] Let $\lambda>0$. Let $X\sim \text{Exp}(\lambda)$ be an exponential random variable with the probability density function $f_X(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbb{R}_+}(x)$. Let $Y$ be an absolutely continuous independent symmetrizer of $X$. Then we have for all $\alpha>0$ that
\begin{equation}
h_\alpha (Y)\ge h_\alpha(X)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
T_\alpha (Y)\ge T_\alpha(X).
\end{equation}
Equality holds if and only if $Y$ is an independent copy of $-X$.

