[Conj actualtype2constant of arXiv:2411.10633, 'A Geometric Perspective on the Injective Norm of Sums of Random Tensors'] Let $\mathcal{C}_{r,p}(d)$ be Type-$2$ constant as in (def:type2constant of the paper)($2 \leq p < \infty$), then 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{C}_{r,p}(d) \underset{r,p}{\lesssim} d^{\frac12- \frac1{\max\{p,r\}}} (\log \, d)^{E_{r,p}}
    \end{equation}
    holds, where $E_{r,p}$ is an exponent only depending on $r$ and $p$. We cannot rule out that the following stronger inequality also holds: 
    $$ \mathcal{C}_{r,p}(d) \underset{r,p}{\lesssim} d^{\frac12- \frac1p} (\log \, d)^{E_{r,p}} .$$ (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For every $\ka>0$, there exists some $\th_\ka>0$ such that
for every $m=1,2$ and $t\in(0,\th_\ka)$ the following sequences of random variables are uniformly integrable:
\[\big\{\msf T_{\ka,\eps}(t)^m\mr e^{-mt\msf c_{\ka,\eps}}:\eps>0\big\}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\big\{\msf M_{\ka,\eps}(t)^m\mr e^{-mt\msf c_{\ka,\eps}}:\eps>0\big\}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Lyapunov exponent of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The upper pth moment Lyapunov exponent is defined as \\
\begin{align}
    \gamma(p):=\limsup_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{t} \ln \sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}^d}E(|u(t,x_0)|^p) \hspace{8mm} \text{for all } p \in (0,\infty).
\end{align}

