[Proposition prop: 2625 of arXiv:2607.05848, 'Mesoscopic eigenvalue statistics for correlated random matrices'] Recall that we fixed $\hat{\epsilon}\in(0,\beta_{*}/3)$ satisfying $[E_{0}-\hat{\epsilon},E_{0}+\hat{\epsilon}]\subset\mathcal{I}(3\epsilon_{0}/2)$.
Consider $z=x+\mathrm{i}\eta_{*}$ and $w=y-\mathrm{i}\eta_{*}$ where $x,y\in[E_{0}-\hat{\epsilon},E_{0}+\hat{\epsilon}]$ and $\eta_{*}=N^{-100}$.
There exists a small constant $c>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
	|\langle L(z,\bar{z}),R(z,\bar{z})M^{-1}(\bar{z})M'(\bar{z})\rangle| > c,
\end{equation}
and, similarly,
\begin{equation*}
	|\langle L(\bar{w},w),M'(\bar{w})M^{-1}(\bar{w})R(\bar{w},w)\rangle| > c.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

