[Lemma lem:weightedPinsker of arXiv:2607.07598, 'Universal Central Limit Theorem for non-exchangeable interacting diffusions'] Let $P$ and $Q$ be probability measures on a measurable space $\mathcal{X}$. Let $\psi\ge0$ be such that $\int_{\mathcal{X}} \psi^2\,\mathrm{d}(P+Q)<\infty$. Then, with $|P-Q|$ denoting the total variation measure of $P-Q$, we have
		\begin{align} 
			\bigg(\int_{\mathcal{X}} \psi\,\mathrm{d}|P-Q|\bigg)^2
			\le
			2\,H(P\,|\,Q)\int_{\mathcal{X}} \psi^2\,\mathrm{d}(P+Q).
		\end{align}

