[Definition 4 (A trajectory of triangles) of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] Given $M \subset M_0$. A \textit{trajectory} on $M$ is an arrangement of triangles in $Tri(M)$ obtained by connecting triangles via common edges. In general, arrangements are non-linear (i.e., they contain branches). $TRAJ(M)$ denotes the collection of all trajectories on $M$, i.e,
\begin{equation*}
TRAJ(M):=\{ \psi \subset Tri(M) \ | \  \text{$\psi$ is a trajectory on $M$}\}.
\end{equation*}
Given $\psi \in TRAJ(M)$ and $abc \in Tri(M)$. \underline{We write $abc \in \psi$ if $abc$ is contained} \underline{in $\psi$}. Given $\psi_1, \psi_2 \in TRAJ(M)$. \underline{We write $\psi_1 \subset \psi_2$ if $abc \in \psi_2$ for $\forall abc \in \psi_1$}.

[Lemma 5 (Lemma 1.2.3, \cite{nualart2006malliavin}.) of arXiv:2506.22213, 'No arbitrage assumption implies the differentiability of derivative pricing function'] Let $(F^n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence of random variables in $\bbD^{1,2}$ that converges to $F$ in $L^2(\Omega)$ and
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_n \bbE\int_0^T|\scD_s F^n|^2ds<\infty.
    \end{align*}
Then $F$ belongs to $\bbD^{1,2}$ and the sequence of derivatives $(\scD F^n)_n$ converges to $\scD F$ in the weak topology of $L^2(\Omega;L^2[0,T])$.

