[Lemma continuous-to-discrete of arXiv:2606.25135, 'Convergence Rates for Semistochastic Processes'] Let $\pi$ denote the stationary distribution for a continuous-time Markov process $\{X_t\}$  with Markov semigroup \, $\mathcal{U}^t$ and let $\Delta t > 0$ be a fixed time increment.  
If we set 
\begin{equation}  
Q= \mathcal{U}^{\Delta t} \ , 
\end{equation}
then for any initial distribution $\mu_0$ of~$X_0$, 
\begin{equation*}
d_{\mathrm{TV}}\left( \mu_0 \, \mathcal{U}^t  ,  \pi \right)   \leq  d_{\mathrm{TV}}\left( \mu_0 \, Q^n  , \pi \right) \ ,
\end{equation*}
where $n=\lfloor t / \Delta t \rfloor$ is the greatest integer less than or equal to $t / \Delta t$\ .

[Theorem of arXiv:2411.16651, 'On a problem of optimal mixing'] Let $X$, $Y$ be Polish spaces, let $\mu_1,\dots,\mu_n$ and $
u$ be atomless probability measures on the corresponding spaces, and let $c:X	imes Y 	o [0,\infty)$ be lower semi-continuous function, then
		$$ \inf\limits_{\pi\in	\Pi(\overrightarrow{\mu},{
u})} K_c(\pi):= \int\limits_{X	imes Y}c(x,y)\; d\pi = \sup\limits_{(\overrightarrow\phi,\psi)\in J(\overrightarrow{\mu},{
u})} \int_X\phi(x) d{\mu} +  \int_Y\psi(y) d{
u}, $$
		where 
		$$ J(\overrightarrow{\mu},{
