   2\delta-\vartheta \in \beta \mathbb{Z}+ \pi\mathbb{Z}).
\end{equation}
If $\frac{\beta}{\pi}\in \mathbb Q$, then $\phi_1$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{C}(y)$ if and only if condition (equation eq:CNS of the paper) is satisfied. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2609.00974, 'On Algebraic Integrability\\ of Vector Fields \\ with Rational Function Coefficients\\ tha'] Let $k$ be a perfect field. 

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Let $f\in k(x)$. Then the inclusion $k(x)\to k((x))$ preserves the value of $\widetilde{[x^{n}]}f$.
        \item Let $f\in k((x))$ and $n\in \{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}$, then if $f=\sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}} a_n x^n$, then
        \begin{equation}
            \widetilde{[x^{n}]}f = \sum_{\substack{m=pk+n,\\ k\in \mathbb{Z}}} x^{pk}a_m.
        \end{equation}
        Consequently, for any $f,g\in k((x))$ we have $\widetilde{[x^{n}]}f+\widetilde{[x^{n}]}g=\widetilde{[x^{n}]}(f+g)$.
        \item Let $f\in k((x)), g\in k((x))^p$, and $n\in \{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}$, then
        \begin{equation}
        \widetilde{[x^{n}]}(gf)=g \cdot \widetilde{[x^{n}]}f.
        \end{equation}
        In particular, if $f\ne0$, then we have
        \begin{equation}
        \widetilde{[x^{p-1}]}f^{p-1}=f^p \cdot \widetilde{[x^{p-1}]}\frac{1}{f}.
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}

[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2512.08595, 'Small time asymptotics of spectral heat content of isotropic processes'] Let $\Omega\subset\R^d$ be a bounded open set with $C^{1,1}$ boundary. Then there exists a bounded $C^{1,1}$ function $\phi:\R^d\to\R$ such that 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item $\Omega = \{x: \phi(x)>0\}$,
			\item $|\nabla \phi(x)|=1$ in a neighborhood of $\partial\Omega$.
		\end{enumerate}

