[Corollary 18 (No endpoint clustering of crossovers) of arXiv:2601.16580, 'Coin flipping and waiting times paradoxes \\ Why fair coins are exceptional'] For any non-equivalent pair $(T_1,T_2)$, $g_{T_1,T_2}$ has only finitely many zeros in $(0,1)$,
and there exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that:
\begin{itemize}\itemsep2pt
\item if $h(T_1)\ne h(T_2)$ then $g_{T_1,T_2}(p)$ has a fixed nonzero sign on $(0,\varepsilon]$;
\item if $t(T_1)\ne t(T_2)$ then $g_{T_1,T_2}(p)$ has a fixed nonzero sign on $[1-\varepsilon,1)$.
\end{itemize}

[Prop partition-fn-bound of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] With the choice of parameters as in (equation eq:N-large-assumption of the paper)-(equation eq:B-choice of the paper), we have that
\begin{equs}
\sup_{K \leq B} e^{-(B-K)} |\phi(\varnothing, K)| \leq \phi(\varnothing, B) = Z_{\Lambda, \beta, N, B}. 
\end{equs}

