{"artifact_digest":"sha256:8f87f5916e5146da5f5df7ad873915b9c266f1af6a1c39f2ff710600cfd0bc3b","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/8f/8f87f5916e5146da5f5df7ad873915b9c266f1af6a1c39f2ff710600cfd0bc3b.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:90055d24c6404c554c5ae8a37af86031644be655b4077666398943fcc2029af6","bundle_entry_key":"2607-08696-lemma-37-tilde-m","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_067","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_067.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-08696-lemma-37-tilde-m","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $\deterministic^{(0)}=V_n$. By \eqref{eq:V-diag}, this matrix is diagonalizable with high probability and the entries of its eigenvectors turn to be approximated, up to a multiplicative error $1+\ohp  \left({\|\centered\|}/{\lambda_l}\right)$, by the eigenvectors of  $ {\rm Diag}(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_r)$, which are given by the canonical basis. As a consequence eigenvectors of $\deterministic^{(0)}$ are approximately orthogonal.
