[Theorem lemma:simple-dominated-above of arXiv:2506.17168, '{\bf Scaling limits for sample autocovariance operators of Hilbert space-valued linear pro'] Let $f \in \mathcal{H}^2$. Then, the $L^2(\mathbb Y^2)$-valued double Wiener-It\^o integral $\mathcal{I}_2(f)$ of the kernel $f$ exists and
    \begin{equation} 
         \operatorname{E} \left( \int_{\mathbb Y^2} |\mathcal{I}_2(f^{(r,s)})|^2 \mu(dr) \mu(ds)\right)^{1/2} < \infty.
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, the sequence $\{f_{(n)}\}_{n \in \mathbb N}$ approximating $f \in \mathcal{H}^2(\mathbb Y^2 \times \mathbb R^2)$ can be selected such that, for all $n \in \mathbb N$,
    \begin{equation} 
        f_{(n)}^{(r,s)}(x,y) \le f^{(r,s)}(x,y), \quad \text{for all } (x,y) \in \mathbb R^2, (r,s) \in \mathbb Y^2.
    \end{equation}

