[Prop construction of arXiv:2505.19622, 'Discrete harmonic polynomials in multidimensional orthants'] Let $\alpha, \beta>0$. We consider the three-dimensional random walk $\mathcal S$ associated to the inventory
\begin{equation}

    \chi_{\mathcal S}(x,y,z) = Q(x,y) + \alpha Q(z,y) + \beta Q(x,z).
\end{equation}
Then its covariance matrix is given by 
\begin{equation}

{\cov}(\chi_{\mathcal S}) =\left( \begin{array}{ccc} 
1 & \frac{- d}{\sqrt{(1+\alpha)(1+\beta)}} &  \frac{- d \beta }{\sqrt{(1+\beta)(\alpha+\beta)}} \\
\frac{- d}{\sqrt{(1+\alpha)(1+\beta)}}  & 1 & \frac{- d \alpha }{\sqrt{(1+\alpha)(\alpha+\beta)}}\\
\frac{-d \beta }{\sqrt{(1+\beta)(\alpha+\beta)}} & \frac{-d \alpha }{\sqrt{(1+\alpha)(\alpha+\beta)} }  & 1 \end{array} \right).
\end{equation}

[Corollary of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] Assume $\mathsf{acc}_k:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is absolutely continuous for each $k\in\mathbb{R}$. \\
		If $\mathsf{acc}$ is strictly proper for all possible random variables $V$, then there is a (strictly) positive function $m$ s.t., for every $k\in\mathbb{R}$ and $x\in \mathbb{R}$, 
		\begin{equation}
			\mathsf{acc}_k(x)=\mathsf{acc}_k(k)-\int_x^k(k-t)m(t)\dd{t}
		\end{equation}
		Moreover, $$m(t)=\frac{\mathsf{acc}_k'(t)}{k-t}$$ for all $k$.

[Lemma Volterra-kernel-TN of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let 
\begin{equation}
K(x,u)=1_{\{0\leq u\leq x\leq \tfrac{1}{2}\}},\qquad (x,u)\in \left[ 0,
\tfrac{1}{2}\right] ^{2}.  
\end{equation}
Then $K$ is a totally nonnegative kernel on $\left[ 0,\tfrac{1}{2}\right]
^{2}$. Equivalently, for every $m\geq 1$ and every strictly increasing
tuples 
\begin{equation}
0<x_{1}<\cdots <x_{m}\leq \frac{1}{2},\qquad 0<u_{1}<\cdots <u_{m}\leq \frac{
1}{2},  
\end{equation}
the determinant 
\begin{equation}
\det \!(K(x_{i},u_{j}))_{i,j=1}^{m}  
\end{equation}
is nonnegative. In fact, each such determinant is either $0$ or $1$.

