[Theorem thm:gs-entire of arXiv:2512.04371, 'Constructive Approximation under Carleman's Condition, \\ with Applications to Smoothed An'] Let $\beta\in (0,1)$.  A Schwartz function $\phi\in\mathcal S(\mathbb{R})$ belongs to 
\(\displaystyle \mathcal S^{\beta}\) 
if and only if it admits an extension to an entire function on~\(\mathbb{C}\) and there exist constants \(C,a>0\) such that
\[
  \bigl|\phi(z)\bigr|
  \;\le\;
  C\,
  \exp\!\bigl(a\,|\mathrm{Im} z|^{\,\frac{1}{1-\beta}}\bigr)
  \quad
  \text{for all }z\in\mathbb{C}.
\]
In other words,
\[
  \mathcal S^{\beta}
  \;=\;
  \Bigl\{
    \phi|_{\mathbb{R}} \in\mathcal S(\mathbb{R})\;:\;
    \phi \;\text{entire and }
    \exists\,a,C>0:\;
    |\phi(z)|\le C\,e^{a|\mathrm{Im} z|^{\!1/(1-\beta)}}
  \Bigr\}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Group $G(\Gamma)$ of arXiv:2609.02123, 'Groups of Transverse Stratifications and the Hierarchy of Configuration Spaces'] Let each vertex $v \in V$ correspond to a codimension-$1$ stratum, and each hyperedge $e \in E$ correspond to a codimension-$2$ stratum where the vertices in $e$ intersect. The group $G(\Gamma)$ is given by generators:
\[
\{a_v \mid v \in V\},
\]
and relations:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a_v^2 = 1$ for all $v \in V$;
\item $(a_{v_1} \cdots a_{v_m})^2 = 1$ for each hyperedge $e = \{v_1,\dots,v_m\} \in E$;
\item commutation relations $a_u a_v = a_v a_u$ when $u$ and $v$ are not contained together in any hyperedge.
\end{itemize}

[Def cps of arXiv:2605.30017, 'Conditional Probability Spaces and the Structure of Agreement\footnote{We thank Emiliano C'] A \textbf{conditional probability space} (\textbf{CPS}) is a pair $(\mathcal G, p)$, where $\mathcal G \subseteq \mathcal F$ is a family of nonempty conditioning events, and $p$ associates with each $G \in \mathcal G$ a probability measure $p_G$ on $\Omega$, satisfying:
\begin{enumerate} [label=(\roman*)]
\item $p_G(G) = 1$ for every $G \in \cal G$;
\item $p_G(E) = p_G(F) \, p_F(E)$ for every $E \subseteq F \subseteq G$ with $E \in \cal F$ and $F, G \in \cal G$.
\end{enumerate}

[Theorem T:biane of arXiv:2412.03521, 'On ergodic properties of stochastic PDEs'] {biane:86:relations}}]
    Let $e = \{e_{\tau}; \tau \in [0,1]\}$ be a 3-$d$ Bessel bridge, and let
    $\zeta$ be a random variable uniformly distributed on $[0,1]$ and
    independent of $e$. Consider a process $\beta = \{\beta_{\tau} ; \tau \in
    [0,1]\}$ defined by
    \begin{align*}
      \beta_{\tau} \coloneqq e_{\tau \oplus \zeta} - e_{\zeta}, \quad \text{with $\tau \oplus \zeta \coloneqq \tau + \zeta\; ({\rm mod}\ 1)$.}
    \end{align*}
    Then $\beta $ is a 1-$d$ Brownian bridge. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 47 (Localized exterior lift and exact area realization) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] Assume first that \(V_T\leq K\) almost surely for a deterministic
\(K<\infty\).  Set
\begin{equation}
 \Xi_0(t)=1,
 \qquad
 \Xi_j(t)=\int_{(0,t]}\Xi_{j-1}(s-)\wedge\mathrm dM_s,
 \qquad
 Z_t^{\lambda,N}=\bigoplus_{j=0}^N\lambda^j\Xi_j(t).

\end{equation}
Then \(Z^{\lambda,N}\) converges to a c\`adl\`ag
\(\mathcal F_{\wedge}(H)\)-valued martingale \(Z^\lambda\) and
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E\sup_{t\leq T}
 \|Z_t^{\lambda,N}-Z_t^\lambda\|^2\longrightarrow0,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E\|Z_T^\lambda\|^2\leq e^{\lambda^2K}.

\end{equation}
Indistinguishably in \(t\),
\begin{equation}
 Z_t^\lambda=(1+\lambda M_t)\wedge
 \exp_{\wedge}(2\lambda^2\alpha_{A_t}).

\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}
 \Xi_{2j}(t)=\frac{2^j}{j!}\alpha_{A_t}^{\wedge j},
 \qquad
 \Xi_{2j+1}(t)=\frac{2^j}{j!}
 M_t\wedge\alpha_{A_t}^{\wedge j},

\end{equation}
and, for \(z\geq0\),
\begin{equation}
 \|P_{\mathrm{even}}Z_t^{\sqrt{z/2}}\|^2
 =\exp(\mathfrak F_{A_t}(z)).

\end{equation}
For an arbitrary square-integrable \(M\), the same assertions hold locally
after a predictable scalar-clock localization.

[Lemma symmetric_f_g of arXiv:2411.11425, 'Non-equilibrium steady states with a spatial Markov structure'] Let $\{\Lambda^n\}_{n \geq 1}$ be a symmetric two-sided Markov family. For arbitrary $z_1,z_2 \in \mathbb{R}^+$, $z_1 \leq z_2$, there exist functions $f,g$ and $h$ on $\mathbb{R}$ such that 

    \begin{equation}
        \Lambda_{a,b}^1(x) = f(a,b) \cdot g(b,x) \cdot h(x,a) \cdot \mathds{1}(x \in I_{a,b})
    \end{equation}

    for all $a,b \in (z_1,z_2)$. Moreover, the functions $f$ and $g$ can be chosen to  be symmetric.

