body:
[Prop potts_gibbs_ldp of arXiv:2609.02682, 'LDP for tensor forms'] Consider the optimization problem (equation eq:potts_unconstrained_opt of the paper), under the assumptions


    \begin{align*}
		W_\infty>0 \,\,\,\lambda\text{-a.e.},\quad \mathcal{V}[W_\infty](x)=1\,\,\lambda\text{-a.e.},\quad
    \text{and} \newline \quad\alpha_r = \theta\geq0, h_r=0 \quad\text{for all  } r\in [c]. 
		\end{align*}
    Then the following conclusions hold:
	\begin{itemize}

		\item[(i)]
		Any optimizer $(x_1,\cdots,x_c)\in \Delta_{c-1}$
		 with $x_1\ge \max_{2\le r\le c}x_r$ has the following form:
		\begin{equation}
			x_1=\frac 1c (1+(c-1)y^*), \quad x_r= \frac 1c (1-y^*), \quad 2 \le r \le c,
		\end{equation}
		for some $0 \le y^* < 1$. 
		\item[(ii)] There exists $\theta_{\rm crit} \in (0,\infty)$ such that, if $\theta<\theta_{\rm crit}$, $(1/c, \ldots, 1/c)$ is the unique optimizer of (equation eq:potts_unconstrained_opt of the paper) and if $\theta>\theta_{\rm crit}$, there exist exactly $c$ distinct optimizers, none of which are uniform. With $(x_{\theta,1},\ldots ,x_{\theta,c})$ denoting the unique optimizer satisfying $x_{\theta,1}>\max_{2\le r\le c} x_{\theta,r}$, the map $\theta\mapsto (x_{\theta,1},\ldots ,x_{\theta,c})$ is continuous on $(\theta_{\rm crit},\infty)$.

	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] Let $\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{ST}}$ be a first-order language (see, e.g., [shoenfield_mathematical_2018]) with a binary predicate symbol $\sqsubset$ and unary predicate symbols $V_p$ for every $p \in \mathord{\mathrm{PropVar}}$.
  For $\varphi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$, and a free variable $x$, the \emph{standard translation} $\mathrm{ST}_x(\varphi)$ is defined inductively:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\bot) = \bot$, $\mathrm{ST}_x(p) = V_p(x)$;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_1 \circledcirc \psi_2) = \mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_1) \circledcirc \mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_2)$, for $\circledcirc \in \set{ \land, \lor, \to }$;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\Box \psi) = \forall y\,(x \sqsubset y \to \mathrm{ST}_y(\psi))$, where $y$ is fresh;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\Diamond \psi) = \exists y\,(x \sqsubset y \land \mathrm{ST}_y(\psi))$, where $y$ is fresh.
  \end{itemize}

[Lemma lem:linear_compactness of arXiv:2608.31071, 'Global smooth solutions to the inhomogeneous Landau--Fermi--Dirac equation'] Suppose that $\mathcal A_n$ and $\mathcal B_n$ are families of measurable coefficients such that: $\mathcal A_n$ are uniformly bounded in $L^\infty([0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3)$; $\mathcal B_n$ are uniformly bounded in $L^2([0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3)$; and
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal A_n(t,x,v) \ge \lambda(t,x,v)\,\mathrm{Id} , \quad \text{where } \quad \lambda(t,x,v)\text{ is uniformly positive on  compact subsets of }(0,T)\times \mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3.
    \end{equation*}
    Further suppose that $f_{\rm in}^n$ is a family of initial data that is uniformly bounded in $L^2_{\rm loc}(\mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3)$. Then, any corresponding family $f_n\colon [0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3$ of weak solutions to (equation eq:linear_kolmogorov of the paper) is locally compact in $L^2_{t,x,v}$. 

    If additionally $\left\langle v \right\rangle^\sigma f_{\rm in}^n$ is uniformly bounded in $L^2_{x,v}$ for some $\sigma > 0$, then $\left\{ f_n \right\}$ is compact in $L^2(0,T;L^2_{x,v})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.21255, 'Laws of the iterated logarithm for random Dirichlet series with general weights'] Let $N$ be a positive integer-valued function satisfying $\lim_{s\to 0+}N(s)=\infty$. For all $\rho>0$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k>N(s)}\frac{|a_k|}{k^{1/2+s}}|\eta_k|\1_{\mathcal{A}_{k,\,\rho}(s)}=0\quad\text{{\rm for all}}~s>0~\text{{\rm close to}}~0\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}};
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\lim_{s\to 0+}s^{\beta/2}\sum_{k>N(s)}\frac{|a_k|}{k^{1/2+s}}\me[|\eta_k|\1_{\mathcal{A}_{k,\,\rho}(s)}]=0.
\end{equation}

