[Lemma lem:global_smooth_max of arXiv:2608.27313, 'A Finite Sample Analysis for Quantile Temporal Difference Learning in Distributional Reinf'] Let $c_{\mathrm g}$ and $\beta$ be defined in
Equation~(equation eq:global_drift_constants of the paper), and let $\Phi_\beta$ be
defined in Equation~(equation eq:smooth_max of the paper). For every
${\bm{e}},{\bm{u}}\in{\mathbb R}^{{\mathcal{S}}\times[m]}$,
\begin{equation}
    \left\|{{\bm{e}}}\right\|_\infty
    \le\Phi_\beta({\bm{e}})
    \le\left\|{{\bm{e}}}\right\|_\infty+\frac{\log(2|{\mathcal{S}}|m)}{\beta},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \left\|{\nabla\Phi_\beta({\bm{e}})}\right\|_1\le1,
    \qquad
    {\bm{u}}^\top\nabla^2\Phi_\beta({\bm{e}}){\bm{u}}
    \le\beta\left\|{{\bm{u}}}\right\|_\infty^2.
\end{equation}
Moreover, if ${\bm{e}}=\bm\theta-\bm\theta_m$ and
$\left\|{{\bm{e}}}\right\|_\infty\ge r_{\mathrm{out}}/2$, then
\begin{equation}
    \left\langle\nabla\Phi_\beta({\bm{e}}),{\bm{h}}(\bm\theta)\right\rangle
    \ge c_{\mathrm g}.
\end{equation}

