[Prop linearEmpiricalFluctuationCLT of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] Assume the hypotheses of (thm:partitionFunctionBound of the paper), including (equation eq:logRateExtraAssumptions of the paper) when $\mathsf{s}=0$.  Fix $\beta\ge0$ and real-valued functions $\phi_1,\ldots,\phi_k\in L^2(\mu)$.
\begingroup

Then, for every $\theta=(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_k)\in{\mathbb{R}}^k$,
\begin{align}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
{\mathbb{E}}_{X_N\sim{\mathbb{Q}}_{N,\beta}(\mu)}
\brak*{\exp\pa*{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu\sum_{i=1}^k\theta_iS_N(\phi_i)}}
=
\exp\pa*{-\frac12\sum_{i,j=1}^k\theta_i\theta_j
\Sigma_{\beta,\mu}(\phi_i,\phi_j)}.
\end{align}
Consequently, $(S_N(\phi_1),\ldots,S_N(\phi_k))$ converges in law under ${\mathbb{Q}}_{N,\beta}(\mu)$ to a centered Gaussian vector with covariance matrix $(\Sigma_{\beta,\mu}(\phi_i,\phi_j))_{i,j=1}^k$.
\endgroup

If, in addition, $\phi_1,\ldots,\phi_k\in L^\infty(\mu)$, then, for every $\theta\in{\mathbb{R}}^k$,
\begin{align}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
{\mathbb{E}}_{X_N\sim{\mathbb{Q}}_{N,\beta}(\mu)}
\brak*{\exp\pa*{\sum_{i=1}^k\theta_iS_N(\phi_i)}}
=
\exp\pa*{\frac12\sum_{i,j=1}^k\theta_i\theta_j
\Sigma_{\beta,\mu}(\phi_i,\phi_j)}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2607.17177, '2607.17177'] For any $T>0$, there exists a constant $C_T>0$ with the following property. For any $\alpha\in(0,1)$, set
\begin{equation}
    N_*(T,\alpha):=\min\left\{N\in\N^+:\mu_{N+1}\geq C_T\alpha^{-2}\right\}.
\end{equation}
If the integer $N$ in \eqref{eq noise oseen} satisfies $N\geq N_*$, then the Markov semigroup $\{P_t\}_{t\geq0}$ associated with equation \eqref{oseen equation} on $\mathcal H$ is $\alpha$-asymptotically regular in time $T$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition lem:Omega of arXiv:2509.17319, 'Non-directed polymers in random environments with range penalties: the high-dimensional ca'] Let ${\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} (r,s)$ be as in (equation eq:Mrs of the paper), then there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for any $t>0$, $p>1$ and $s\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
{\ensuremath{\mathbbm P}} \Bigg(\max\limits_{S\in {\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} (r,s) }\Omega_{r}({\ensuremath{\mathcal R}} _N(S))>r^{\frac{d}{\alpha}}st\Bigg)\\
\le C t^{-\frac{\alpha d}{\alpha+d}}.
\end{equation}

