[Theorem thm:DimConnComp of arXiv:2508.08150, 'Recent Progress on Fractal Percolation'] {BromanCamiaJoostenMeester_DimConnectedComponent} }]
For any $d\geq 2$, $N\geq 2$ and $p\geq p_d(N)$, almost surely
\begin{equation*}
	\dim_{\mathrm{B}}  F^c = \dim_{\mathrm{B}}  F =\dim_{\mathrm{H}}  F.
\end{equation*}
If $ F \neq \varnothing$ then almost surely
\begin{equation*}
	\dim_{\mathrm{H}}  F^c<\dim_{\mathrm{H}} F = \dim_{\mathrm{H}}  F^d.
\end{equation*}
Moreover, for every $p$ there exists $1 \leq \beta=\beta(p) \leq d$ such that
\begin{equation*}
	\mathbb{P}_p\left( F^c=\varnothing \text { or } \dim_{\mathrm{H}}  F^c=\beta\right)=1.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $A,B\subset\mathbb{R}^m$ be finite sets and let $\alpha\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^A$ and $\beta\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^B$. Consider the diagonal embedding $\Delta:\mathbb{R}^m\to \mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^m$, that is $\Delta(x):=(x,x)$. Then, in law, we have the equality
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{E}_\alpha\odot\mathcal{E}_\beta=\left(\mathcal{E}_\alpha\otimes\mathcal{E}_\beta\right)\circ\Delta.
  \end{equation}

