body:
[Corollary corollaryatthebeginning of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] Let $ \mu_{k,n}$, with $k,n\geq 1$ be centered probability measures with uniformly bounded supports, such that 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\int_{\mathbb{R}}x^2\mu_{k,n}(dx)=1,
\end{align*}
for all $n\geq 1$. Denote by $q\geq 1$ be the largest integer such that $m_{j}[\nu]=m_{j}[\mathfrak{g}[\nu]]$, for all $j\leq q$. Then, we can find a  constant $C>0$, independent of $n$, such that for large enough $n$, 
\begin{equation}
d_{W}(\mu_{1,n}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus \mu_{n,n},\mathbf{s}) \leq C\sum_{k=1}^nm_{q+1}[\mu_{k,n}].
\end{equation}
In particular, if the $\mu_{k,n}$ are constant over $k$, 
\begin{equation}
d_{W}(\mu_{1,n}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus \mu_{n,n},\mathbf{s}) \leq Cn^{-(q-1)/2}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

