[Lemma lem_animal of arXiv:2608.08532, 'Constrained Maximum Weight Paths in Random Geometric Graphs'] Suppose the conditions in Theorem~(thm_path of the paper) hold and  let~\(F_c(.)\) be the edge weight ccdf. There are constants~\(\delta_1,\delta_2 > 0\) such that for any~\(x,y > 0\) and~\(Q \geq 1,\)
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left(\max_{\cal P} N_{edge}\left(x,{\cal P}\right) \geq y\right) \leq e^{\delta_1Q \Lambda} \cdot e^{-y} + e^{-\delta_2 N}, 
\end{equation}
where~\(\Lambda := \max(1,t^2N^2F_c(x))\) and the maximum is over all~\(t-\)animals of size~\(Q\) containing the origin. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Intersection patterns/shapes of arXiv:2608.12415, 'Sharp Phase Transition for Ellipsoid Fitting'] Given two shapes $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ that are composable, we define an intersection pattern for $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ to be a non-empty set $I \subseteq \left(V(\tau_1) \setminus V_{\tau_1}\right) \times \left(V(\tau_2) \setminus U_{\tau_2}\right)$ of intersection edges where each vertex is incident to at most one edge. Whenever we have an intersection edge $e = \{a,b\}$ (between vertices of the same type), this means that when we consider maps $\sigma: V(\tau_1) \cup V(\tau_2) \to [n]\cup [d] $, we have the restriction that $\sigma(a) = \sigma(b)$.

We define $\tau_{I} = \tau_{I}(\tau_1\cdot \tau_2)$ to be the shape obtained from $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Start with the properly concatenated shape $\tau_1\circ \tau_2$;
\item For each intersection edge $e = \{a,b\}$, merge $a$ and $b$ together into one vertex.
\end{enumerate}

[Definition eq:defannealedgaussian of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] A potential $V : \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}} \to \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ is said to be an annealed Gaussian potential if the map $k \mapsto k^2 e^{-V(k)}$ is summable over $\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$ and if there exists a Borel probability measure $\mu_V$ on $(0,\infty)$ such that, for any $x \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$,

\begin{equation} 
    e^{-V(x)} = \int_0^\infty e^{- \frac{x^2}{2J}} \mu_V(d J).
\end{equation}

[Lemma le:scalinv of arXiv:2411.09058, 'Time-dependent averages of a critical long-range stochastic heat equation'] For any radius $R>0$,
    any time $t\geq 0$, and any $\varepsilon >0$, we have
\begin{equation}
            u_{t}(B_R)=\varepsilon^{-d/2} u_{\varepsilon t}(B_{\sqrt{\varepsilon}R}) \quad \text{in law}.
    \end{equation}

