[Definition Corner-flip dynamics of arXiv:2412.04032, 'Cutoff for the mixing time of the Facilitated Exclusion Process'] For $\zeta \in \Omega_{K,P}$ and $k \in \mathbb{T}_K$, define $\zeta^k$ the configuration with a flip at $k$ such that 
\begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
        \zeta^k_l =\zeta_l \quad \forall \, l \neq k\\
        \zeta^k_k = \zeta_{k+1} + \zeta_{k-1} - \zeta_k.
    \end{cases} 
    \end{equation}
    Then, the corner-flip dynamics on $\Omega_{K,P}$ is the Markov chain such that for all $k$, $\zeta$ goes to $\zeta^k$ at rate 1 and other transitions are not possible.

[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2605.22760, 'Extremes of Gaussian fields with a product term in the variance'] Assume \ref{ass:variance}--\ref{ass:nondegenerate}, and let \(M>0\).  For every \(\varepsilon>0\), there exists \(\delta>0\) such that, as \(u\to\infty\),
\begin{align}
&\PP\left\{\sup_{\bft\in[0,\delta]^2}X(\bft)>u\right\}
\notag\\
&\quad\le
(1+\varepsilon)\Psi(u)
\biggl[
\calH_\alpha(M)^2
+2\frac{\calH_\alpha(M)^2}{M}u^{2/\alpha}
       \int_0^\delta e^{-(1-\varepsilon)u^2x^\beta}\dd x
\notag\\
&\hspace{7.5em}
+\frac{\calH_\alpha(M)^2}{M^2}u^{4/\alpha}
       \int_0^\delta\int_0^\delta e^{-(1-\varepsilon)u^2V(x,y)}\dd x\dd y
\biggr].
\end{align}
Furthermore, define
\[
        I_\delta(u)=\int_0^\delta\int_0^\delta e^{-u^2V(x,y)}\dd x\dd y .
\]
If \(R_u\to\infty\) and \(R_uq_u=o(u^{-2/\beta})\), then for some function \(\eta(M)\downarrow0\) as \(M\to\infty\),
\begin{align}
&\PP\left\{\sup_{\bft\in[0,\delta]^2}X(\bft)>u\right\}
\notag\\
&\quad\ge
(1-\varepsilon)\frac{\calH_\alpha(M)^2}{M^2}u^{4/\alpha}
\Psi(u)
\int_{R_uq_u}^\delta\int_{R_uq_u}^\delta
      e^{-(1+\varepsilon)u^2V(x,y)}\dd x\dd y
\notag\\
&\hspace{2.5em}
-\eta(M)\Psi(u)u^{4/\alpha}
\int_0^\delta\int_0^\delta e^{-(1-\varepsilon)u^2V(x,y)}\dd x\dd y
+o\left(\Psi(u)u^{4/\alpha}I_\delta(u)\right).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2605.31471, 'Convergence Rates of Continuous-Time Random Walks to Time-Fractional Diffusions with Unbou'] Under Assumption \ref{asm:heavy-tail}, the following estimate holds
        \begin{equation}
            \int_0^\infty | P(\Phi^h_t > a) - P(\hat\Sigma_t > a)| dt \leq (1 + a + a^{-1}) C(\beta) h^{\chi(\beta)},
        \end{equation} 
        where $a>0$, and $C(\beta) > 0, \, \chi(\beta) \in (0, 1)$ can be found explicitly. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 49 (Factor capture and positive exhaustion) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] One has
\begin{equation}
 0\le\widehat N_q\le QR^2\Id.

\end{equation}
Applying the top-eigenspace rule to
$\widehat E_q=R_q^*\widehat N_qR_q$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{a,q}\norm{P_{\widehat V_*}r_{a,q}}^2
 \ge
 \widehat\kappa_{Q,d}
 \frac{\widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)}{R^2},
 \qquad
 \boxed{\widehat\kappa_{Q,d}=\frac1{dQ^2}}.

\end{equation}
At extraction level $J$, stop when
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)
 \le
 \widehat\delta_J
 :=
 \frac{dQ^2R^4}{J}.

\end{equation}
Fewer than $J$ promotions occur, and along the nested trajectory
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{j\ge0}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[j]},r^{[j]})
 \le dQ^2R^4,
 \qquad
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[J]},r^{[J]})
 \longrightarrow0.

\end{equation}
Consequently every proper residual--residual raw contraction and every
active--residual raw contraction tends to zero, while residual covariance is retained.

[Lem lem-rw-harmonic of arXiv:2506.18827, 'Random walk reflected off of infinity, with applications to uniform spanning forests and s'] Let $\mathcal A\subset \mathcal V\mathcal G$ be a finite set and let $\phi : \mathcal A\to\mathbbm R$. Let $h^\phi : \mathcal V\mathcal G\to\mathbbm R$ be the energy-minimizing function with $h^\phi|_{\mathcal A} = \phi$, as in Proposition~(prop-min-harmonic of the paper). Let
\begin{equation*}
\tau_n := \min\{j \geq 0 : Y^n_j \in\mathcal A\} . 
\end{equation*}
For each $n\in\mathbbm N$ such that $\mathcal A\subset\mathcal G_n$ and each choice of starting point $x \in\mathcal V\mathcal G_n$, 
\begin{equation*}
h^\phi(x) = \mathbbm E_x\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ \phi(X^n_{\tau_n} ) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] .
\end{equation*}

